\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Using The C++ Objects}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The C++ objects make using the speech API objects from C++ very easy, especially for developers who aren't experienced COM programmers.

\par The C++ objects included with the SDK are C++ objects for accessing the speech API objects. Develope
rs can use these instead of the COM interfaces if they are unfamiliar with COM. The C++ objects also have some additional functionality to reduce function calls. All of the source code for the C++ objects is included in the speech SDK. Developers might wi
sh to customize the objects or create new objects based upon the old ones. Also, if the documentation is unclear about a function's behavior, developers can look at the source code.
\par }{\b Note}{: To use the C++ objects provided, you should:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{Include <spchwrap.h>. The application doesn't need to include <speech.h> if <spchwrap.h> is included. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
If an application has link problems where some GUIDs are not found, include <initguid.h> in one of your .cpp files. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Link to spchwrap.lib. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Call CoInitialize() when the application starts up. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Create the C++ wrappers using "new". 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Call Init() for every object created by the application to initialize its parameters. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Only use Unicode strings with the speech objects. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Call CoUninitialize() when the application shuts down. Make sure that all of the C++ objects are destroyed before calling CoUninitialize(). 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Most of the function calls are the same as the COM versions but they are all directly accessible from the main object. If there are differences, they are documented.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 ANSI and Unicode Versions of Interfaces and Structures (COM only)}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This applies to the COM interfaces only. The C++ wrappers only support Unicode.
\par In the speech API, every COM interface and structure that uses strings, and every structure that contains strings, actually
 has two versions: ANSI and Unicode. Each version is identified by a one-character suffix to the interface or structure name: A for ANSI and W for Unicode. (This is similar to the way Windows NT handles ANSI/Unicode portability.) For example, the two vers
ions of the }{\b IAudioSource}{ interface are IAudioSourceA for ANSI and IAudioSourceW for Unicode.
\par The ANSI version of an interface or structure supports characters (WCHAR) as one-byte ANSI or multibyte characters. The Unicode version supports characters as two-byte Unicode. The two interfaces have different GUIDs.
\par Typically, an application does not need to specify which version to use, because the base name of the interface or structure is automatically mapped to either ANSI or Unicode as follows:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{The name of an interface or structure is ordinarily mapped to the ANSI version. For example, an application that specifies }{\b SRMODEINFO}{ gets SRMODEINFOA. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
If the application declares Unicode as its default (#define _S_UNICODE), the name of the interface or structure is mapped to the Unicode version. In this case, an application that specifies }{\b SRMODEINFO}{ gets SRMODEINFOW. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If the application needs the version that is not automatically mapped, it must specify that version explicitly.
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~about Unicode in Windows-based applications, see the Win32 SDK.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Common Pointer Behavior and Rules}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
NULL pointers and handles are documented for each function where they are acceptable. If NULL is passed to a function where it is not acceptable, the function returns an E_INVAL
IDARG error value. Invalid handles or non-NULL pointers that point to invalid memory cause undefined behavior. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Threads}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Speech objects use the OLE apartment threading model, which permits the creation of processes containing more than one thread of executi
on. Apartment threading rules are simple, but they must be followed carefully:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{Every object should execute on only one thread (within a single apartment). 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
The OleInitialize or CoInitialize function must be called to initialize each thread. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {All pointers t
o objects must be marshaled between apartments (ensuring that all calls to an object are made on its thread). 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Each apartment with objects in it must have a message queue to handle calls from other processes and apartments within the same process. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {Apartme
nt-aware in-process objects must be registered as such in the system registry (that is, the "ThreadingModel = Apartment" entry is required). 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Apartment-aware objects must write dynamic-link library (DLL) entry-point functions carefully. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Multiple objects c
an execute on a single thread, but no object can execute on more than one thread. In addition, it is necessary to call OleInitialize or CoInitialize on each thread, because each thread requires the services of the OLE library.
\par The apartment threading rules have the following implications for application and engine developers:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{The application must create all notification sinks on the same thread that creates the audio objects and engines. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Engines must call all notification sinks on the thread in which they were created. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Objects (with the exception of the apartment-aware DLL entry-point functions) do not need to be thread safe because they will always be called from the thread in which they were created. 
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~about OLE and OLE COM, see the Win32 SDK.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Avoiding Remote Procedure Call Exceptions}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
An application can cause an RPC_E_CANTCALLOUT_ININPUTSYNCCALL exception if it calls OLE functions from within a message handler. This exception occurs when the message handler calls a local server while
 processing a message that was generated by another process. The message can be the result of the process calling the SendMessage function directly, or calling a function that caused the system to send the message. For example, the RPC_E_CANTCALLOUT_ININP
UTSYNCCALL exception would occur if an application calls the }{\b IVCmdMenu}{::Activate member function while handling the WM_ACTIVATE message. 
\par To avoid this problem, an application should use its message handler to post itself a private message (WM_USER + n), and then call OLE functions from the code that handles the private message. The following example illustrates this technique. 
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 LRESULT CALLBACK MyWindowProc (
\par     HWND hWnd, UINT uMsg,
\par     WPARAM wParam,
\par     LPARAM lParam)
\par \{
\par     switch (uMsg) \{
\par         case WM_ACTIVATE:
\par             .
\par             . // perform typical WM_ACTIVATE tasks
\par             .
\par                 // Post a private message instead of calling OLE functions.
\par                 PostMessage (hWnd, WM_MYACTIVATE, wParam, lParam);
\par                 return 0;
\par             case WM_MYACTIVATE:
\par                 pVoiceInterface->Activate(hWnd, NULL);
\par                 return 0;
\par             .
\par             . //..process other messages
\par             .
\par     \}
\par \}}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A slight variation of this technique is implemented in the vo
ice object (VOICEOBJ.CPP and VOICEOBJ.H) that is part of the VOICEMDI sample in the Microsoft Speech SDK. See especially the CVoiceMenuObject::Activate, CVoiceMenuObject::ActivateMsg, CVoiceMenuObject::Deactivate, and CVoiceMenuObject::DeactivateMsg funct
ions. 
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 SAPI Basics}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Voice Command}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The Voice Command interface is the high-level interface for speech recognition. It is designed to provide command and control speech recognition for applications. With this inte
rface, a user gives the computer simple commands, such as "Open the file," and can answer simple yes/no questions. Command and Control does not allow speech dictation.
\par The Voice Command design mimics a Windows menu in behavior, providing a "menu" of comman
ds that users can speak. Basically, to use voice commands, an application designs a Voice menu that corresponds to a window or state within the application. Most programs will have one Voice menu for the main window and one for every dialog box. Contained
 
within every Voice menu is a list of voice commands that users can say. When they say one, the application is notified which command was spoken. "Open a file" and "Send mail to <e-mail name>" are typical voice commands. Each voice command has information 
in addition to the spoken command, such as a description string and a command ID.
\par Voice commands allow the user to control an application by speaking commands through an audio input device rather than by using the mouse or keyboard, giving the user hands-fr
ee control of the application. Voice commands involve the use of an audio input device, such as a microphone or a telephone, a speech recognition engine, and a voice menu. When the user speaks a command into the audio input device, the speech recognition 
e
ngine attempts to transcribe the spoken input into text. If the engine succeeds, it compares the command text to that of the commands in the active voice menus. (A voice menu contains a list of commands to which an application can respond.) If the engine 
finds a matching command in a voice menu, it notifies the application of the match, and the application carries out the command. 
\par Some users do not wish to have the computer speak or to talk to the computer. For these users, Microsoft provides a Speech cont
rol panel application that sets a registry entry indicating the user's preferences. An application can check this entry before trying to load its Voice Command code and can save memory by not loading the code if it is not needed.
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~about the registry entry, see Registry Entries.
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 SAPI Basics}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Voice Dictation}{
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Version_3_0}Version 3.0}{{\*\bkmkend Version_3_0}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Voice Dictation requires version 3.0 or higher of the speech API. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Why_Use_Voice_Dictation_}Why Use Voice Dictation?}{
{\*\bkmkend Why_Use_Voice_Dictation_}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The "Voice Dictation" module all
ows users to dictate into applications. It uses the metaphor of an invisible edit box to describe how the application receives and displays text from a user's speech.
\par The "Voice Dictation" object employs the invisible (or you might prefer to think of it as
 virtual) edit box to receive the text whenever the user speaks a word. The application employing the Voice Dictation object is then notified that the text in the edit box has changed, and how it has changed, so that it can display any changes to the user
. 
\par Voice Dictation provides considerably more functionality than mere transcription. It provides automatic text formatting (capitalization, spacing), translation of punctuation words into symbols, built-in glossary entries, limited commands, and a GUI so us
ers can correct the dictation engine. It has the potential to support more functionality in the future, especially when continuous dictation becomes widespread. Thus, an application will most likely maintain a GUI of its own with a rich-text control that 
can be kept synchronized with the invisible edit box, displaying formatted and punctuated text from dictation.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart About_Voice_Dictation}About Voice Dictation}{
{\*\bkmkend About_Voice_Dictation}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Some elements of Voice Dictation that you should know of are:
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sb120\sa120\sl-400\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f17\fs36\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Topics}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Speech lends itself to classification by topic. An appl
ication should have a unique "topic" for each classification of document that might be dictated. Example topics might be "E-mail", "Formal Writing", or "C-code" (these classifications use drastically different styles of language). Voice Dictation will sto
re information on topics away so that any information the recognizer learns will be stored, along with any glossary entries that the user generates specifically for the topic.
\par Adding a new topic might take a lot of hard disk or memory, so unless an application has a very good reason for creating a new topic, it should try to use an existing topic. Topics cannot be merged.
\par Much of the information stored within a topic data structures is engine dependent so switching dictation engine vendors may cause a loss of some data.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sb120\sa120\sl-400\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f17\fs36\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Dictation Session}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Whenever an application wishes to use dictation, it must instantiate a "Dictation Session." Each session must be based upon a "Topic" and have a window handle associated with it. The dictation session will only be actively li
stening when that window has the keyboard focus. It is possible for an application to have multiple sessions, or multiple applications each to have their own sessions. As a user switches between the applications he/she will be dictating into a different s
ession.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sb120\sa120\sl-400\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f17\fs36\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Correction Window}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The application can also have the dictation object provide some GUI, although the application doesn't necessarily need the GUI.
\par The "Correction Window" displays some user-interface that allows the user to correct the last word spok
en and/or the currently selected word. It will look something like this: (The Correction Window GUI may look different for continuous dictation systems.)
\par }\pard \s16\sa120\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\field{\*\fldinst {PRIVATE "TYPE=PICT;ALT=Correction Window"}}{\fldrslt }}{\lang1024 {\*\shppict
