\fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Interaction_with_Voice_Commands}
Interaction with Voice Commands}{{\*\bkmkend Interaction_with_Voice_Commands}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Voice Dictation will use the speech recognition sharing object. It will share a speech recognition engine with voice-commands if the engine can support both dictation and continuous CFGs. If the engine supports this, users will be able to have dictatio
n and command and control listening at the same time. If two separate engines are used then Voice Dictation and voice commands will be mutually exclusive, so turning on one will turn off the other. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Reducing_Memory_Footprint}Reducing Memory Footprint}{
{\*\bkmkend Reducing_Memory_Footprint}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The Voice Dictation object main
tains a mirror image of the text that is in the document. If a user loads in a large document, this might get unwieldy, especially if audio recordings are maintained for each of the words. Audio data is inherently large. Here are some ways to alleviate so
me of the memory problems: 1? The application can call }{\b IVDctAttributes}{
::MemorySet() to specify how much memory should be used by storing the audio and other speech recognition correction information. Of course, if hundreds of pages are being mirrored, there
's a minimum amount of memory that will be consumed. 2? Alternatively, an application might only keep the last page or paragraph that the user has dictated in the Voice Dictation buffer. This places a cap on the size of the data and may work well for the 
user. However, if the user scrolls back a few pages and wishes to hear what he/she said then they might be disappointed because the information won't be available. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Localization_Issues}Localization Issues}{{\*\bkmkend Localization_Issues
}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {For the Voice Dictation object to correctly recognize a specific languag
e it must be supplied with an engine supporting the specific language. Most features of the Voice Dictation object will work without any localization of the object, but some features require localization.
\par Features that need localization:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{Default Global Glossary- If no default table for the global glossary exist for the specific language then nothing will be loaded in. An application or user could add their own in. Punctuation is handled by the glossary. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Built-In Commands -- If a translation table for the built-in commands does not exist then the English-named commands will be loaded in. An application or user could rename them into the vernacular. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Inverse Text Normalization -- If the localized default CFG is not available then the application will need to set one. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 SAPI Basics}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Voice Text}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Voice text is a feature that provides simple text-to-speech capabilities for Windows-based applications. You use voice text to convert words in a character string into speech played over the computer speaker or a telephone line. 
\par Implementing voice text in an application involves the use of the Voice Text object. The object manages the interaction between the text-to-speech engine that creates the speech and the computer speaker
 or telephone line that serves as the destination for the speech. Before an application can begin using voice text, it must create an instance of the Voice Text object and register itself with the object. This section describes the Voice Text object and e
xplains how to use it to implement voice text in an application. 
\par Some users do not wish to have the computer talk or to talk to the computer. For these users, Microsoft provides a speech control panel application that sets a registry entry indicating the u
ser's preferences. An application can check this entry before trying to load its voice commands code, and save memory by not loading the code if it is not needed. 
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~about the registry entry, see Registry Entries.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Voice_Text_Site}Voice-Text Site}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A tex
t-to-speech engine plays voice text through a Voice Text site on the user's machine. A Voice Text site consists of an audio-output device, such as the computer speaker or a telephone line, and a text-to-speech mode. An engine typically provides several di
fferent modes for playing speech, each representing a different voice. Each voice plays text in a different speaking style or personality. 
\par An application must create a separate Voice Text object for each site that it needs to use. To use a site other than 
the default (the computer speaker and the default mode of the engine), the application must obtain the identifier of the desired audio-output device and the globally unique identifier (GUID) of the desired mode, and then pass that information to the Voice
 Text object when registering. 
\par An application uses the waveOutGetNumDevs and waveOutGetDevCaps multimedia functions to obtain the device identifier of an audio-output device. To determine the available text-to-speech modes, an application creates a text-to
-speech enumerator. The enumerator returns information about the modes provided by all engines in the user's computer, including the GUID of each mode. 
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~about multimedia and multimedia functions, see the documentation included with t
he Microsoft Win32 Software Development Kit (SDK). 
\par }{\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~about enumerating the modes of a text-to-speech engine, see DirectTextToSpeech API Reference. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {{\*\bkmkend Voice_Text_Site}
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 SAPI Basics}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Voice Telephony}{
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Telephony_Controls}Telephony Controls}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Microsoft Windows has a concept known as "Controls." Windows controls are buttons, list boxes, sliders, and other manipulatable elements on a dialog box. The controls display some graphics on the screen, accept a variety of inputs, keyboard or mou
se, and pass the information to the application. Many controls are running simultaneously, and to are asynchronous. A user could twiddle one control, type into another one, and return back to the previous one. For the most part, the application cannot act
 on the input from the controls until the dialog box returns.
\par A "telephony control" is the code that executes a specific type of sub-dialogue in a conversational dialogue. Telephony controls are different than Windows controls. They are someplace in between
 a Windows control and a Windows dialog box. While both telephony controls and windows provide the application with information from the user, the similarity ends there. Telephony controls cannot run simultaneously with other telephony controls (on the sa
m
e phone call), and they do not retain the same asynchronous characteristics as a windows control. When a telephony control returns information to the application it's not possible for the user to change his/her mind unless the application presents the tel
ephony control again. With a Windows control, the user can continue to change the control until he/she presses the "OK" button on the dialog box.
\par Windows provides a set of standard Windows controls: push buttons, radio buttons, check boxes, list boxes, edit
 controls, sliders, etc. Applications combine these together to make a dialog box. (Sometimes applications write their own controls. I'll explain that later.) The Microsoft Speech API is similar. It provides a standard set of telephony controls: Yes/No, p
hone number, digits, etc. The application can combine these, along with its own telephony controls, to produce a dialogue with the user.
\par The standard telephony controls are:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{Yes/No -- This asks the user a question, such as "Do you want to buy the plane tick
et?" It waits for a yes or no response, handling all the variations such as "Okay" and "You betcha". It also handles error conditions, such as the user failing to respond. When it's done it returns a "yes", "no", or "problem" result to the application. 

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {Ph
one number and extension -- The phone number control asks the user for a phone number. Once the user has spoken a number it then asks for verification since recognition of digits has a fairly high error rate. Like all of the other controls, it handles err
or conditions such as no response and unrecognized responses. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Date -- Asks the user to speak a date, either in absolute terms, such as January 4th, or relative, such as "tomorrow." 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Time -- Asks the user to speak a time. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Record Audio -- Records digital audio. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Grammar -- Asks the user a question and listens for a response using an arbitrary grammar.
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
When an application uses telephony controls it designs a synchronous system that leads sequentially from one control to another. A pseudo-code program might look like:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 AskYesNo ("Do you want to pay with a credit card?");
\par If (Yes) \{
\par \tab AskList ("With Master Card, Visa, or American Express?",
\par \tab \tab \tab "Master Card", "Visa", "American Express");
\par \tab if (Master Card)
\par \tab \tab AskMasterCardNumber();
\par \tab else if (Visa)
\par \tab \tab AskVisaNumber();
\par \tab else if (American Express)
\par \tab \tab AskAmExNumber();
\par \tab else /* problem */
\par \tab \tab SendToOperator();
\par \} else if (No) \{
\par \tab TellUserWhereToSendCheck();
\par \} else /* problem */ \{
\par \tab SendCallToOperator();
\par \}}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To a large extent, programming a telephony user interface is similar to programming a command line program with printf() and scanf().

\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Telephony}Telephony Information Object}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
All of the telephony controls use a COM object called the "Telephony Information Object." The object is basically a repository of information about the telephone line and call that the control can use. The telephony information object stores:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{A list of interfaces to other objects. It has interfaces for the speech recognition engine, text-to-speech queue, and logging object. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
A set of parameters, such as flags indicating if the telephony line supports full duplex. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Application or control specific information.
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The telephony object also provides some useful telephony functions for notifying the controls that DTMF was sent, creating an audio source/destination connected to the line, and a function to quickly initialize the object with data. 
\par {\*\bkmkend Telephony_Controls}{\*\bkmkend Telephony}
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 SAPI Basics}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 DirectSpeechRecognition}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When an application uses the low-level speech recognition interfaces, it is talking directly to the engi
ne. This provides the application with much more control but also involves more work. Because the low-level API is more complex, this article won't go into detail about how the engine object is used. However, an architectural overview will give you an ide
a of the processes involved.
\par The low-level API consists of many more objects than the high-level interface (voice commands). Here's how the process works:
\par The application determines where the speech recognition's audio should come from and creates an audio-
source object through which the engine acquires the data. Microsoft supplies an audio-source object that gets its audio from the multimedia wave-in device, but the application is able to use customized audio sources, such as an audio source which acquires
 audio from a .WAV file or a specialized hardware device. 
\par The application, through a speech recognition enumerator object (provided by Microsoft), locates a speech recognition engine that it wants to use. It then creates an instance of the engine object and passes it the audio-source object. 
\par The engine object has a dialog with the audio-source object to find a common data format for the digital audio data, such as pulse code modulation (PCM). Once a suitable format is established, the engine creates an aud
io-source notification sink that it passes to the audio-source object. From then on, the audio-source object submits digital audio data to the engine through the notification sink. 
\par The application can then register a main notification sink that receives grammar-independent notifications, such as whether or not the user is speaking. 
\par When it is ready, the application creates one or more grammar objects. These are similar to the Voice Menu object in voice commands but more flexible in syntax recognition. 
\par To
 find out what words the user spoke, the application creates a grammar-notification sink for every grammar object. When the grammar object recognizes a word or phrase, or has other grammar-specific information for the application, it calls functions in th
e grammar-notification sinks. The application responds to the notifications and takes whatever actions are necessary. 
\par Typically, when a grammar object recognizes speech, it sends the grammar-notification sink a string indicating what was spoken. However, t
he engine may know a lot more information than this, such as alternative phrases that may have been spoken, timing, or even who is speaking the phrase. An application can find this information by requesting a results object for the phrase and interrogatin
g the results object 
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 SAPI Basics}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 DirectTextToSpeech}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
When an application uses the low-level text-to-speech interfaces, it is talking directly to the engine. As with speech recognition, this provides the applica
tion with a lot more control, but requires more work of it. Because the low-level API is more complex, this document won't go into detail about how objects are used. However, an architectural overview follows.
\par The low-level API consists of many more objects than the high-level API (voice text). Here's how the process works: 
\par The application determines where the text-to-speech audio should be sent and creates an audio-destination object through which the engine sends the data. Microsoft supplies an audio-des
tination object that sends its audio to the multimedia wave-out device, but the application may use customized audio destinations, such as an audio destination that writes to a .WAV file. 
\par The application, through a text-to-speech enumerator object (provided by Microsoft), locates a text-to-speech engine and voice that it wants to use. It then creates an instance of the engine object and passes it the audio-destination object. 
\par The engine object has a dialog with the audio-destination object to find a commo
n data format for the digital audio. Once an acceptable format is established, the engine creates an Audio-Destination Notification Sink that it passes to the audio-destination object. From then on, the audio-destination object submits status information 
to the engine through the notification sink. 
\par The application can then register a Main Notification Sink that receives buffer-independent notifications, such as whether the synthesized voice is speaking and mouth positions for animation. 
\par When it is ready, the application passes one or more text buffers to the engine. These will be queued and then spoken (to the audio destination) by the engine. 
\par To find out what words are currently being spoken, the application can create a Buffer Notification Sink for ever
y buffer object. When the engine speaks a word, reaches a bookmark, or some other event occurs, it calls functions in the Buffer Notification Sinks. The notification sink is released when the buffer has been read. 
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 SAPI Basics}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Audio Objects }{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Application developers who incorporate speech recognition or text-to-speech features in their applications need to supply an audio source or audio destination object. The audio source object is required for speech recogniti
on applications--it tells the engine where to get audio from. Audio destination objects are similarly required by text-to-speech applications. Microsoft provides a set of audio objects that might be useful to applications. Of course, applications are free
 to provide their own audio source and destination objects, including those from 3rd party vendors.
