\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In this reference, the reference information provided for each me
mber function in the speech API includes a list of error values that the function can return if it fails. In addition to these values, the member functions supported by the higher level objects can return error values that were actually generated by a low
e
r level component. For example, an audio-source object can generate error values that can be passed to a speech recognition object, and then to a voice-command object. This passing of error values applies to all functions in the speech API except for thos
e provided by the audio object, which is the lowest level of the API.
\par Because speech components are local servers instead of in-process servers, it is possible for a speech function to return an error value that does not map to any of the hexadecimal error 
values that appear in the header files. This is because a system component has mapped the error value to an HRESULT by using the HRESULT_FROM_WIN32 macro. 
\par If a speech function returns an error value that does not seem to be in the OLE range, you should st
rip off the bits added by the HRESULT_FROM_WIN32 macro, convert the error value to decimal, and then check for a non-OLE error value that matches the returned error value. 
\par The following errors may be returned by common member functions. If no error occurs, the member functions return NOERROR.
