\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Speech-recognition engines and text-to-speech engines provide dialog boxes that enable the user to configure the engine. An application can direct an engine to display one of its dialog boxes by using the }{\b IVCmdDialogs}{ or }{\b IVTxtDialogs}{
 interface. However, if a voice-navigation application, such as Microsoft Voice, is running, an application does not need use these interfaces because the voice-navigation application gives the user access to the engine's dialog boxes. 
\par At a minimum, a voice-navigation application has the following characteristics:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{Allows the user to turn voice commands and voice text on and off and allows access to engine dialog boxes and attribute settings, such as the awake and enabled states. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Provides feedback that tells the user whether the voice-command or Voice Text capability is on or off and, optionally, the current state of the voice-command and Voice Text objects. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An applica
tion should assume that a voice-navigation application is always present in the system. An application can determine if a voice-navigation application is running by checking the value of the UseSpeech registry entry located under HKEY_CURRENT_USER\\
Software\\
Voice key. UseSpeech is TRUE if a voice-navigation application is running. If UseSpeech if FALSE or not present in the registry, there is no voice-navigation application. (The UseSpeech entry is not present if no speech software is installed in the system
.)
\par When the user turns speech on or off, the voice-navigation application must change the value of the UseSpeech entry and broadcast the WM_SPEECHSTARTED or WM_SPEECHENDED message to all top-level windows. Changing the entry and broadcasting the messages i
s best accomplished by using a control panel application.
\par If UseSpeech is FALSE when an application starts, but the application requires speech to be useful, it should display a dialog box asking the user to turn on speech and then exit. An application tha
t is useful even without speech features should just start without loading its speech code.
\par If UseSpeech is TRUE when the application starts, it should use the current attribute settings of its voice-command and Voice Text sites and defer changing settings
 to the voice-navigation application. For sites other than the system speaker, the application should provide an interface that allows the user to adjust the attributes.
\par You can provide your own voice-navigation application, or you can rely on one supplied
 by a third party for voice-navigation features. Applications that only use telephony features or send speech output to a device other than the primary sound card can ignore the UseSpeech entry. 
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Microsoft Speech API 4.0}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 International Phonetic Alphabet}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA) is a standard system for indicating specific sounds, or phonemes. The Unicode character set contains all single symbols and diacritics in the most recently published IPA 
revision, as well as a few symbols no longer in use.
\par If an engine supports the IPA, an application can adjust the pronunciation of words by passing to the engine the IPA phonemes that represent the correct pronunciation, expressed as Unicode values.
\par This section lists the Unicode values for common IPA phonemes. An application developer can use the information in this section to adjust pronunciations used by a speech recognition or text-to-speech engine that supports the IPA. 
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Microsoft Speech API 4.0}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Determining Whether an Engine Supports the IPA}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To determine whether a text-to-speech engine supports the IPA, check the dwFeatures member of the }{\b TTSMODEINFO}{
 structure that contains information about a text-to-speech mode of that engine. If dwFeatures includes TTSFEATURE_IPAUNICODE, the mode supports the IPA.
\par To determine whether a speech recognition engine supports the IPA, check whether it supports the }{\b ILexPronounce}{ interface. If so, it also supports the IPA, because the interface requires it.
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Microsoft Speech API 4.0}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Adjusting an Engine's Pronunciation of a Word}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Typically, it should not be necessary to adjust the pronunciations of words in the engine's native language. 
However, engines are likely to mispronounce foreign names or invented words (such as those that commonly occur in game software). An application can adjust an engine's pronunciation of a word in any of the following ways:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{If the engine has a pronunciation lexicon, the correct pronunciation can be added to the lexicon and the incorrect pronunciation (if any) removed. \line \line }{\b For more information}{ see the }{\b ILexPronounce}{ interface in this section.\line 

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
The application can provide a text-to-speech engine with phonemes instead of ASCII text to be spoken. \line \line }{\b For more information}{ see the }{\b ITTSCentral}{::TextData member function in the DirectTextToSpeech API Reference. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {For example, suppose a text-to-speech engine mispronounces a word that is not in the engine's lexicon. Correcting
 the pronunciation would involve the following steps:
\par Determine what phonemes the engine is using by calling the }{\b ITTSCentral}{::Phoneme member function with the text of the word. For example: 
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li1080\ri1080\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 pITTSCentral-Phoneme(CHARSET_IPAPHONETIC, //IPA character set

\par     NULL,       // text is not tagged
\par     dtext,      // text of the word
\par     pdPhoneme); // function fills in pointer to phonemes}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The function fills in the }{\b SDATA}{ structure pointed to by the pdPhoneme parameter with a pointer to a WCHAR array of Unicode value
s, which contains the phonemes used to pronounce the word. 
\par Check the phonemes to determine which ones should be changed. 
\par Store the correct pronunciation in the engine's lexicon by calling the }{\b ILexPronounce}{::Add member function, or pass the corrected phonemes to the engine by calling the }{\b ITTSCentral}{
::TextData member function and specifying CHARSET_IPAPHONETIC for the eCharacterSet parameter. 
\par If an engine doesn't support the IPA or the }{\b ILexPronounce}{ interface, it may still provide a dialog box that allows an end-user to adjust pronunciation, typically by supporting the LexiconDlg function of the }{\b ISRDialogs}{
 interface (for a speech recognition engine) or the }{\b ITTSDialogs}{ interface (for a text-to-speech engine). 
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx720{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~see DirectSpeechRecognition API Reference or DirectTextToSpeech API Reference.
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Microsoft Speech API 4.0}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Phonetic Alphabets}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Every text-to-speech and speech recognition engine uses its own phonetic character set, because standardized ch
aracter sets are usually not as complete as necessary for the vendor's purposes. The vendor defines the way engine-specific phonemes map to standardized character sets.
\par The International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA) is a standard phonetic character set that is 
already supported in Unicode. Although the IPA is not used by any specific engine, it is an international standard. At least a rudimentary conversion between the engine's character set and the IPA set is possible, although the conversion may lose some cha
racters. (It is unlikely that any engine will use this set internally.)
\par The IPA character set is defined in the Unicode standard. It occupies 0x0250 through 0x02AF for the sounds and some Latin characters, and 0x02B0 through 0x02FF for the modifier characte
rs. If an engine's flags indicate that it understands the IPA, an application can use IPA characters with that engine.
\par Because of the size of the IPA character set, the only way to send IPA characters to or receive them from an engine is through Unicode. T
here is no 8-bit method of using a standard phonetic set. You can use the Phoneme Conversion object in Speech Tools to display an 8-bit phoneme set that is language specific.
\par The IPA standard considers its characters to be a separate alphabet that includes
 the lowercase Latin characters "a" through "z" and other symbols. IPA Latin characters are not duplicated in Unicode, so many IPA characters are found outside the standard phonetic and modifier blocks. 
\par Because the IPA is primarily used by linguists to ca
pture spoken language in print, IPA characters in the standard phonetic block are arranged in order of their resemblance to the Latin characters "a" through "z" and not their phonetic similarity. As a result, adjacent Unicode values may bear no relation t
o each other phonetically. 
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Microsoft Speech API 4.0}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Unicode Values for IPA Characters}{
