\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This section demonstrates how to perform the following tasks: 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{Initialize an application to use voice commands. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Enumerate voice menus. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Add commands to a voice menu. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {Re
spond to Voice Command notifications. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {For more coding examples, see the sample applications provided with the SDK.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Initializing}Initializing An Application To Use Voice Commands}{
{\*\bkmkend Initializing}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To use voice commands, you need to create a Voice Command object, register your applicatio
n with the object, and then create a Voice Menu object to manage your application's voice menus. You create a Voice Command object by calling the }{\b CoCreateInstance}{
 function with the CLSID_VCmd class identifier and the IID_IVoiceCmd interface identifier. You must create a separate Voice Command object for each site that your application needs to use. 
\par }{\b CoCreateInstance}{ returns a pointer to the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{ interface for the Voice Command object. Before it can perform other Voice Command tasks, an application must register itself by calling the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b Register}{
 member function. }{\b Register}{ specifies the site that the object represents and passes the address of the application's Voice Command notification interface to the Voice Command object.
\par After creating a Voice Command object and registering the application, you can use the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b MenuCreate}{ member function to open a voice menu and create a Voice Menu object to represent the menu. }{\b MenuCreate}{
 retrieves the address of the }{\b IVCmdMenu}{ interface for the Voice Menu object. You can use the interface's member functions to manage the menu and its commands.
\par The following example shows how to create a Voice Command object, register an application, and create a Voice Menu object. The example creates a temporary Voice Menu
 object; that is, the object is not added to the Voice Menu database maintained by the Voice Command object.
\par The function initializes OLE, creates an instance of the Voice Command object, registers the application with the object, and creates a temporary V
oice Menu object. It returns TRUE if successful or FALSE otherwise. The function uses the following global variables and constants:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\i gpIVoiceCommmand}{ -- address of the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{ interface for the Voice Command object 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {\i gpIVCmdDialogs
}{ -- address of the }{\b IVCmdDialogs}{ interface for the Voice Command object 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {\i gpIVCmdMenu}{
 -- address of the }{\b IVCmdMenu}{ interface for the Voice Menu object 
\par }\trowd \trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf11 \cltxlrtb \cellx7320\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\intbl
\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\field{\*\fldinst {PRIVATE}{{\*\datafield 
020203001a0200000000017e0000000000000000000001000000000000000000cf1f010000001a00000001000000000000000000000000000000010000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000d61454048a1554041b000000b21554041c000000cf1f0000000000008c00000000000000000000000000
00000000000000000000000000000000}}}{\fldrslt }}{\cs82 
\par BOOL BeginOLE (void)
\par \{
\par     HRESULT  hRes;
\par     VCMDNAME VCMDNAME;
\par     LANGUAGE language;
\par     PIVCmdAttributes  pIVCmdAttributes;
\par     // Initialize interface pointers to NULL.
\par     gpIVoiceCommand = NULL;
\par     gpIVCmdDialogs = NULL;
\par     gpIVCmdMenu = NULL;
\par     // Initialize OLE.
\par     if (FAILED(CoInitialize(NULL)))
\par         return FALSE;
\par     // Create the Voice Command object.
\par     if (CoCreateInstance(CLSID_VCmd, NULL, CLSCTX_LOCAL_SERVER,
\par             IID_IVoiceCmd, (LPVOID *) &gpIVoiceCommand) != S_OK)
\par         goto fail;
\par     // Retrieve the address of the Voice Command object's IVCmdDialogs
\par     // interface. The application uses this interface to direct the
\par     // speech recognition engine to display its configuration dialog boxes.
\par     hRes = gpIVoiceCommand-QueryInterface(IID_IVCmdDialogs,
\par         (LPVOID FAR *) &gpIVCmdDialogs);
\par     if (hRes)
\par         goto fail;
\par     // Register the application with the Voice Command object.
\par     hRes = gpIVoiceCommand->Register(
\par         "",               // use the default site (computer microphone)
\par         &gVCmdNotifySink, // address of application's notification interface
\par         IID_IVCmdNotifySink, // GUID of the notification interface
\par         VCMDRF_ALLMESSAGES,  // send all notifications to the interface
\par         NULL);               // do not change the site settings
\par     if (hRes)
\par         goto fail;
\par     // Use the voice command's IVCmdAttributes interface to ensure that
\par     // speech recognition is awake and enabled for the whole system.
\par     hRes = gpIVoiceCommand-QueryInterface(IID_IVCmdAttributes,
\par         (LPVOID FAR *) &pIVCmdAttributes);
\par     if (pIVCmdAttributes) \{
\par         pIVCmdAttributes-EnabledSet(TRUE);
\par         pIVCmdAttributes-AwakeStateSet(TRUE);
\par         pIVCmdAttributes-Release();
\par         \};
\par     // Create a Voice Menu object.
\par     lstrcpy (VCMDNAME.szApplication, "My Demo Application");
\par     lstrcpy (VCMDNAME.szState, "Main");
\par     Language.LanguageID = LANG_ENGLISH;
\par     lstrcpy (Language.szDialect, "US English");
\par     hRes = gpIVoiceCommand->MenuCreate(
\par         &VCMDNAME,          // application name and state
\par         &LANGUAGE,          // language and dialect
\par         VCMDMC_CREATE_TEMP, // temporary menu (not stored in database)
\par         &gpIVCmdMenu        // receives address of Voice Menu object
\par         );
\par     if (hRes)
\par         goto fail;
\par     return TRUE;
\par     // If the BeginOLE function fails, release the IVoiceCmd, IVCmdDialogs,
\par     // and IVCmdMenu interfaces and set the interface pointers to NULL.
\par     fail:
\par         if (gpIVoiceCommand)
\par             gpIVoiceCommand->Release();
\par         if (gpIVCmdDialogs)
\par             gpIVCmdDialogs->Release();
\par         if (gpIVCmdMenu)
\par             gpIVCmdMenu->Release();
\par         gpIVoiceCommand = NULL;
\par         gpIVCmdDialogs = NULL;
\par         gpIVCmdMenu = NULL;
\par         return FALSE;
\par \}}{\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Enumerating}Enumerating Voice Menus}{{\*\bkmkend Enumerating}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The Voice Command object maintains a database of voice menus that have been created by applications
 using voice commands. You can identify the voice menus in the database by using a Voice Menu enumerator, which you create by using the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b MenuEnum}{ member function. When calling }{\b MenuEnum}{
, you specify whether to enumerate all menus in the database, or only those belonging to a particular application and state. You also specify whether to enumerate active menus, permanent menus, open menus, and so on. 
\par }{\b MenuEnum}{ returns a pointer to an }{\b IVCmdEnum}{ interface. You use the member functions of this interface to identify the menus that match the enumeration criteria specified in the call to }{\b MenuEnum}{. You use the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b 
MenuCreate}{ member function to create a Voice Menu object for a menu that you retrieved from the database.
\par The following example creates a Voice Menu enumerator and uses it to identify menus in the database belonging to an application called "My Demo Application."
\par The function uses a Voice Menu enumerator to find an application's menus in the Voice Menu database. It returns TRUE if successful or FALSE otherwise.
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\intbl\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\field{\*\fldinst {PRIVATE}{{\*\datafield 
020203001a0200000000017e0000000000000000000001000000000000000000cf1f010000001a01000001000000000000000000000000000000010000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000b81e5404db1e54041b010000f91e54041c010000cf1f0000000000008c00000000000000000000000000
00000000000000000000000000000000}}}{\fldrslt }}{\cs82 
\par BOOL DoEnum(void) \{
\par     HRESULT hRes;
\par     VCMDNAME VCName;
\par     PIVCmdEnum pIVCmdEnum = NULL;
\par 
\par     // Create a Voice Menu enumerator object.
\par     hRes = gpIVoiceCommand->MenuEnum(
\par         VCMDEF_DATABASE,       // enumerate menus in database
\par         "My Demo Application", // enumerate menus for demo application
\par         NULL,                  // enumerate menus for all states
\par         &pIVCmdEnum);          // receives address of enumerator
\par     if (hRes)
\par         goto fail;
\par     while (S_OK == pIVCmdEnum->Next(1, &VCName, NULL))
\par         // DoMenuObject is an application-defined function that uses
\par         // MenuCreate to create a Voice Menu object.
\par         DoMenuObject(&VCName);
\par fail:
\par     // Release the enumerator.
\par     if (pIVCmdEnum)
\par         pIVCmdEnum-Release();
\par     pIVCmdEnum = NULL;
\par     if (hRes)
\par         return FALSE;
\par     else
\par         return TRUE;
\par \}}{\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Adding}Adding Commands to a Voice Menu}{{\*\bkmkend Adding}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {After you create a Voice Menu object, you can add commands to the menu by filling an array of }{\b VCMDCOMMAND}{
 structures, copying the address and size of the array into an }{\b SDATA}{ structure, and passing the address of the }{\b SDATA}{ structure to the }{\b IVCmdMenu}{::}{\b Add}{ member function. 
\par The example in this section shows how to add a new set of commands to a Voice Menu object. The example consists of three functions: }{\b UseCommands}{, }{\b GetCommands}{, and }{\b NextCommand}{:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{The }{\b UseCommands}{ function deactivates the voice menu, replaces any existing commands in the menu with a new set, and reactivates the menu. One of the parameters to }{\b UseCommands}{
 is the address of a buffer containing the list of command strings to enter. }{\b UseCommands}{ passes the address of the command-string buffer to the }{\b GetCommands}{ function, along with the address of an }{\b SDATA}{ structure. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {The }{\b 
GetCommands}{ function converts the buffer to an array of }{\b VCMDCOMMAND}{ structures and copies the address and size of the array into the }{\b SDATA}{ structure. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {The }{\b 
NextCommand}{ function is a helper routine that }{\b GetCommands}{ uses to retrieve individual command strings from the command buffer passed to }{\b UseCommands}{. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b UseCommands}{
 adds commands to a Voice Menu object, replacing any existing commands. It Returns the result code returned by a Voice Menu function. }{\i pszCommands}{ is the address of a null-terminated string of commands separated by \\n or \\r characters. }{\i pMenu}
{ is the address of the }{\b IVCmdMenu}{ interface for the Voice Menu object.
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\intbl\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\field{\*\fldinst {PRIVATE}{{\*\datafield 
020203001a0200000000017e0000000000000000000001000000000000000000cf1f010000001a02000001000000000000000000000000000000010000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000e8f3af050bf4af051b02000029f4af051c020000cf1f0000000000008c00000000000000000000000000
00000000000000000000000000000000}}}{\fldrslt }}{\cs82 
\par HRESULT UseCommands (char *pszCommands, PIVCmdMenu pMenu)
\par \{
\par     HRESULT hRes;
\par     SDATA   data;
\par     DWORD   dwNum, dwStart;
\par     hRes = pMenu->Deactivate(); // deactivate the menu
\par     if (hRes) return hRes;
\par     // Retrieve the number of commands in the menu.
\par     hRes = pMenu->Num(&dwNum);
\par     if (hRes) return hRes;
\par     // Remove the existing commands from the menu.
\par     if (dwNum)
\par        hRes = pMenu->Remove (1, dwNum, VCMD_BY_POSITION);
\par     if (hRes) return hRes;
\par     // Call GetCommands, an application-defined function that fills an SDATA
\par     // structure with information about the commands to add to the menu.
\par     if (!GetCommands(pszCommands, &data, &dwNum))
\par        return ResultFromScode (E_OUTOFMEMORY);
\par     // Add the commands to the menu.
\par     hRes = pMenu->Add (dwNum, data, &dwStart);
\par     if (hRes) return hRes;
\par     // Free the command data.
\par     free (data.pData);
\par     // Reactivate the menu.
\par     hRes = pMenu->Activate(ghWndDialog, 0);
\par     return hRes;
\par \}}{\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b GetCommands}
{ takes a block of memory containing command strings and converts it into a list of }{\b VCMDCOMMAND}{ structures. It returns TRUE if successful or FALSE otherwise. }{\i pszMemory}{ is the address of a null-terminated string of commands separated by \\
n or \\r characters. }{\i pData}{ is the address of an }{\b SDATA}{ structure that receives the address and size of the memory containing the }{\b VCMDCOMMAND}{ structures. The caller must free the memory by using the }{\b free}{ function. }{\i 
pdwNumCommands}{ receives the count of }{\b VCMDCOMMAND}{ structures in the memory block
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\intbl\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\field{\*\fldinst {PRIVATE}{{\*\datafield 
020203001a0200000000017e0000000000000000000001000000000000000000cf1f010000001a03000001000000000000000000000000000000010000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000a3f7af05c6f7af051b030000e4f7af051c030000cf1f0000000000008c00000000000000000000000000
00000000000000000000000000000000}}}{\fldrslt }}{\cs82 
\par BOOL GetCommands(char *pszMemory, PSDATA pData, DWORD *pdwNumCommands)
\par \{
\par     PSTR  pTemp;
\par     DWORD dwTotal, dwSize, dwSizeDesc, dwSizeCat;
\par     DWORD dwSizeCmd;
\par     PVCMDCOMMAND pCmd, pCmdNew;
\par     CHAR    *pszBegin;
\par     DWORD   dwCmdSize;
\par     DWORD   dwCmds = 0;  // current count
\par     DWORD   dwCount = 1; // command number
\par     char    szCat[] = "Main";
\par     dwTotal = dwSize = 0;
\par     pTemp = (PSTR) malloc(0);
\par     if (!pTemp)
\par         return FALSE;
\par     pCmd = (PVCMDCOMMAND) pTemp;
\par     for( ;; ) \{
\par         // Retrieve the next command from the list of command strings.
\par         pszMemory = NextCommand (pszMemory, &pszBegin, &dwCmdSize);
\par         if (!pszMemory)
\par             break;   // no more
\par         dwSize = sizeof(VCMDCOMMAND); // size of header
\par         dwSizeCmd = (dwCmdSize + 1);  // get command-string length
\par         // Align the command string on a doubleword boundary.
\par         dwSizeCmd += 3;
\par         dwSizeCmd &= (~3);
\par         dwSize += dwSizeCmd;
\par         dwSizeDesc = (dwCmdSize + 1); // get description length
\par         // Align the description on a doubleword boundary.
\par         dwSizeDesc += 3;
\par         dwSizeDesc &= (~3);
\par         dwSize += dwSizeDesc;
\par         dwSizeCat = lstrlen(szCat) + 1; // get category length
\par         // Align the category string on a doubleword boundary.
\par         dwSizeCat += 3;
\par         dwSizeCat &= (~3);
\par         dwSize += dwSizeCat;
\par         dwSize += sizeof(DWORD); // action indicator
\par         // Accumulate the total size of the command.
\par         dwTotal += dwSize;
\par         // Reallocate enough memory to hold the command.
\par         pTemp = (PSTR) realloc((PVOID) pCmd, dwTotal);
\par         // Fill the new VCMDCOMMAND structure.
\par         pCmd = (PVCMDCOMMAND) pTemp;
\par         pTemp += (dwTotal-dwSize);
\par         pCmdNew = (PVCMDCOMMAND) pTemp;
\par         memset (pCmdNew, 0, dwSize);
\par         pCmdNew->dwSize = dwSize;
\par         pCmdNew->dwFlags = 0;
\par         pCmdNew->dwAction = (DWORD) (pCmdNew->abData -- (PBYTE) pTemp);
\par         pCmdNew->dwActionSize = sizeof(DWORD);
\par         pCmdNew->dwCommandText = NULL;
\par         // Point past the header to the beginning of the command data.
\par         pTemp += (pCmdNew->abData -- (PBYTE) pTemp);
\par         // Set the action index.
\par         *(DWORD *) pTemp = dwCount++;
\par         pTemp += sizeof(DWORD);
\par         // Set the command string.
\par         pCmdNew->dwCommand = (DWORD) ((PBYTE) pTemp -- (PBYTE) pCmdNew);
\par         strncpy(pTemp, pszBegin, dwCmdSize);
\par         pTemp += dwSizeCmd;
\par         // Set the description.
\par         pCmdNew->dwDescription =
\par             (DWORD) ((PBYTE) pTemp -- (PBYTE) pCmdNew);
\par         strncpy(pTemp, pszBegin, dwCmdSize);
\par         pTemp += dwSizeDesc;
\par         // Set the category.
\par         pCmdNew->dwCategory = (DWORD) ((PBYTE) pTemp -- (PBYTE) pCmdNew);
\par         strcpy(pTemp, szCat);
\par         // Increment the count of commands.
\par         dwCmds++;
\par     \}
\par     pData-pData = (PVOID) pCmd;
\par     pData-dwSize = dwTotal;
\par     *pdwNumCommands = dwCmds;
\par     return TRUE;
\par \}}{\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b NextCommand}
{ finds the next command in a block of memory. It returns the address of the next command string in the block, or NULL if no command string is found. }{\i pszMemory}{ is the address of a null-terminated string of commands separated by \\n or \\
r characters. }{\i pBegin}{ receives the address of the next command string. }{\i pdwSize}{ receives the number of bytes in the command string, not including any terminating NULL character.
\par }\trowd \trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf11 \cltxlrtb \cellx7202\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\intbl
\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\field{\*\fldinst {PRIVATE}{{\*\datafield 
020203001a0200000000017e0000000000000000000001000000000000000000cf1f010000001a040000010000000000000000000000000000000100000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000005f905f0482905f041b040000a0905f041c040000cf1f0000000000008c00000000000000000000000000
00000000000000000000000000000000}}}{\fldrslt }}{\cs82 
\par CHAR * NextCommand (CHAR *pszMemory, PCHAR *pBegin, DWORD *pdwSize)
\par \{
\par     DWORD i;
\par     for( ;; ) \{
\par         // Try to find a non-newline character.
\par         while ((*pszMemory == '\\n') || (*pszMemory == '\\r')) \{
\par             if (*pszMemory == '\\0')
\par                 return NULL;
\par             pszMemory++;
\par         \};
\par     // Try to find a newline character.
\par     for (i = 0;
\par         (pszMemory[i] != '\\n') && (pszMemory[i] != '\\r') &&
\par             (pszMemory[i] != '\\0'); i++);
\par     if (!i) \{
\par         if (!pszMemory[i])
\par             return NULL; // end
\par         pszMemory++;
\par         continue;        // try again
\par       \};
\par     // A string was found. Return it.
\par     *pBegin = pszMemory;
\par     *pdwSize = i;
\par     return pszMemory + i;
\par     \};
\par \}}{\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Responding}Responding To Voice Command Notifications}{{\*\bkmkend Responding}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The Voice Command object calls an application's }{\b IVCmdNotifySink}{ interface to inform the application of Voice Command events so 
that the application can respond to them. To receive notifications, the application must create a COM object that supports the }{\b IVCmdNotifySink}{ interface and must pass the address of the interface to the Voice Command object when calling the }{\b 
IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b Register}{ function. 
\par The }{\b IVCmdNotifySink}{ interface consists of a set of member functions that correspond to Voice Command events. When an event occurs on the site that the application is using, the Voice Command object calls the member function that corresponds
 to the event. 
\par The following example shows how to define an object class that implements the }{\b IVCmdNotifySink}{ interface.
\par }\trowd \trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf11 \cltxlrtb \cellx7320\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\intbl
