\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Implementing Voice Commands in an application involves the use of four different objects:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{Voice Command object 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {Notification sink
\line The notification sink code is supplied by the application. The voice-Voice Command object calls methods in this object both when commands are spoken and for VU-level updates. It is up to the application to act upon the notifications. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {Voice menu 

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
Voice-menu enumerator 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Voice Command API }{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Voice_Command_COM_Object}Voice Command COM Object}{
{\*\bkmkend Voice_Command_COM_Object}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
This is the central control point for the Voice Command module. Most applications will use only this as a means to create Voice Menu objects or Voice Menu enumerators. 
\par The Voice Command object manages the use of voice commands by all voice-aware applica
tions in the system. It manages the interaction between applications and the speech recognition engine. It also manages aspects of the interaction between the engine and the audio input device. Before an application can begin using voice commands, it must
 create an instance of the Voice Command object and register itself with the object. 
\par A speech recognition engine receives spoken audio input from the user through a Voice Command site on the user's computer. A Voice Command site consists of an audio input 
device, such as a microphone or telephone, and a speech recognition mode. (An engine typically provides several different modes for recognizing speech, each representing a different language or dialect.)
\par An application must create a separate Voice Command 
object for each Voice Command site it needs to use. To use a site other than the default (the computer microphone and the default mode of the engine), the application must obtain the identifier of the desired audio input device and the globally unique ide
ntifier (GUID) of the desired mode and then pass that information to the Voice Command object when registering. 
\par An application can use the waveInGetNumDevs and waveInGetDevCaps multimedia functions to obtain the device identifier of an audio input device. 
To determine the available speech recognition modes, an application creates a speech recognition enumerator. The enumerator returns information about all modes provided by all engines in the user's system, including the GUID of each mode. 
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~about multimedia and multimedia functions, see the Microsoft Win32 Software Development Kit (SDK). 

\par }{\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~about enumerating the modes of a speech recognition engine, see DirectSpeechRecognition API Reference.
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The Voice Command objec
t can use any speech recognition engine that supports lists. An engine that does not support lists cannot be used for voice commands.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Created by}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To create a Voice Command object, an application calls the CoCreateInstance function with the CLSID_VCmd class 
identifier and the IID_IVoiceCmd interface identifier. An application must create a Voice Command object for each audio source that it needs to use. For example, if the application needs to recognize commands from a telephone line as well as the computer 
microphone, it should create two Voice Command objects.
\par After creating a Voice Command object, CoCreateInstance returns a pointer to the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{ interface for the Voice Command object. An application uses }{\b IVoiceCmd}{ to register itself with the speech recog
nition engine and to create Voice Menu objects and Voice Menu enumerators.
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~
about Voice Menu objects and Voice Menu enumerators, see Voice Menu COM Object and Voice Menu Enumerator COM Object.
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Before an application can perform other Voice Command tasks, it must register itself by calling the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b Register}{
 member function. By registering, the application tells the engine which site to use as the source of voice commands and passes the Voice Command object a pointer to the applicat
ion's Voice Command notification interface. The object calls the notification interface to notify the application about events related to voice commands. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Interacts with}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The Voice Command object calls an application's }{\b IVCmdNotifySink}{ interface to notify th
e application of speech-related events. An application must create a COM object that supports the }{\b IVCmdNotifySink}{ interface and must pass the address of the interface to the Voice Command object when calling the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b Register}{
 member function. The application developer determines how the functions in the interface respond to notifications.
\par An application creates a Voice Menu object by using the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b MenuCreate}{ member function. It can enumerate available voice menus by using a Voice Menu enumerator created by the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b MenuEnum}{ member function.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Interfaces}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The Voice Command object supports the following interfaces:
\par }\trowd \trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf11 \cltxlrtb \cellx1379\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf11 \cltxlrtb 
