\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Voice_Menu_COM_Object}Voice Menu COM Object}{
{\*\bkmkend Voice_Menu_COM_Object}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The Voice Menu object provides most of the controls for speech recognition and is similar to a Windows-menu object accessed with an HMENU. The object allows commands to be added or removed from the menu and to have speech recognition 
activated (and listening) or deactivated. 
\par A Voice Menu object has an interface that an application uses to manage its voice menus. A voice menu contains a set of commands that a speech recognition engine can recognize and that the application can respond 
to when the menu is active. An application must create a Voice Menu object to represent a voice menu; only then can the engine use the menu to recognize commands. An engine can use more than one voice menu for an application, but the application must crea
te a separate Voice Menu object for each menu. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Created by}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {After an application has registered its site with the Voice Command object, it can create a Voice Menu object by using the }{\b IVoiceCmd}
{::}{\b MenuCreate}{ member function. MenuCreate retrieves a pointer to an }{\b IVCmdMenu}{ interface for the newly created Voice Menu object. An application uses the }{\b IVCmdMenu}{
 member functions to obtain information about the voice menu, add and remove menu commands, and so on.
\par When calling MenuCreate, the application specifies the nam
e of the voice menu that the new object is to represent and indicates whether the function should create a new menu or open an existing one from the Voice Menu database. If the function creates a new menu, the menu initially contains no commands. The appl
ication must add commands by using the }{\b IVCmdMenu}{::}{\b Add}{ member function. 
\par A voice menu is stored in the database, unless it was created as a temporary menu. A temporary menu is destroyed when the Voice Menu object is released. Typically, you will store a voic
e menu between invocations of the Voice Command object, so you should first call MenuCreate with the VCMDMC_OPEN_EXISTING value. If MenuCreate returns an error, you should call it a second time with the VCMDMC_OPEN_ALWAYS value, which creates a new voice 
menu for the application.
\par An application can use the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b MenuEnum}{ member function to create a Voice Menu enumerator object to enumerate the menus currently in the Voice Menu database.
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~about Voice Menu enumerators, see Voice Menu Enumerator COM Object.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Interface}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The Voice Menu object supports the }{\b IVCmdMenu}{
 interface, which allows an application to activate or deactivate a voice menu, request training for the menu, or define the commands in a voice menu, including retrieving and setting lists to use within the context of a command.
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Voice Command API }{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Voice_Menu_Enumerator_COM_Object}Voice Menu Enumerator COM Object}{
{\*\bkmkend Voice_Menu_Enumerator_COM_Object}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A Voice Menu enumerator enumerates the voice menus stored in the Voice Menu database. Applications use t
he enumerator to obtain the list of Voice menus that are stored in the internal database and to determine which of these menus are active. Most applications will not use the Voice Menu enumerator. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Created by}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You create a Voice Menu enumerator by using the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b MenuEnum}{ member function. MenuEnum returns a pointer to an }{\b 
IVCmdEnum}{ interface on the object. When calling MenuEnum, you can enumerate only menus that are already open, only those that are active, or all menus in the database.
\par The Voice Menu enumerator persists until all references to it are released, even if the Voice Command object is released first.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Interacts with}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An application uses the OLE enumeration functions Clone, Next, Reset, and Skip to obtain the address of a }{\b VCMDNAME}{ structure that 
identifies the desired voice menu. The application passes the address of the structure to the }{\b IVoiceCmd}{::}{\b MenuCreate}{ member function to create a Voice Menu object.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Interface}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The Voice Menu enumerator supports the }{\b IVCmdEnum}{ interface, which enumerates the vo
ice menus stored in the Voice Menu database, using }{\b VCMDNAME}{ structures as the elements in the enumeration sequence.
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Voice Command API }{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 COM Interfaces}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This section describes, in alphabetical order, the interfaces and functions that support Voice Commands.
\par In addition to the interfaces described in this section, each object supports }{\b IUnknown}{, which is a common interface supported by all OLE component objects. A speech recognition engine may also support }{\b ILexPronounce}{ or }{\b ILexPronounce2}{
, optional interfaces that allow a user to correct words in the engine's pronunciation lexicon. 
\par Voice Command interfaces contain functions that allow an application to implement simple speech recognition, or command and control. There are the following interfaces:
