\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\cf1 An application can use the }{\b\cf1 ListSet}{\cf1  member function to associate any number of }{\i\cf1 phrase lists}{\cf1 
 with a voice menu. A }{\i\cf1 phrase list}{\cf1 
 is a named set of words or phrases. By embedding the name of a list in the command string, the user can speak any word or phrase in the list at the given point in a command string. For example, a list named "myfriends" might contain the name
s "Linda," "Tom," and "Mary." If the list name were embedded in a command string, such as "Send mail to <myfriends>," the user could speak "Send mail to Linda," Send mail to Tom," or "Send mail to Mary."
\par Typically, the list contains information that change
s dynamically at run time, such as the ten people to whom the user most recently sent electronic mail. For best results, a list should have fewer than 20 entries. Having more than 20 entries in a list can reduce the accuracy of recognition.
\par The list persists until the Voice Menu object is released. List entries are not automatically saved to disk. To preserve the list, call the }{\b\cf1 ListGet}{\cf1  member function and take steps to store the result.
\par }{\b\cf1 ListSet}{\cf1  is much faster than the }{\b\cf1 IVCmdMenu}{\cf1  interface's }{\b\cf1 Add}{\cf1 , }{\b\cf1 Remove}{\cf1 , or }{\b\cf1 Set}{\cf1 
 member functions because list entries are substituted when a command is recognized and the menu is not recompiled. This means that }{\b\cf1 ListSet}{\cf1  can be called on an active menu without affecting performance.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Num}Num}{{\*\bkmkend Num}
