\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The Voice Dictation grammar is used by Voice Dictation to perform inverse text normalization. The data is a text string terminated by a NULL. It contains several sections, each section designated by a word between brackets. The sections are:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{[Space] \line The [space] section defines spacing rules for the topic, determining how many spaces are inserted between words. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {[Capital] \line 
The capitalization rules tell the system when to capitalize the first letter of a word automatically. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {[Rules] \line 
The [rules] section contains rules for the context free grammar. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {[Switch] \line 
The [switches] section provides human readable descriptions of the option switches, with one switch per line. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The SDK provides a Default Grammar for English.
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Voice Dictation API }{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 [Space]}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The space section contains a series of lines (separated by "\\n" or "\\n\\
r"). Each line represents a "rule" that determines if a space is used between two words (or symbols.) The parser analyzes each rule in priority from first to last.
\par Each line contains three "strings", optionally followed by some commands in curly braces. The first string indicates what text occurs to the left of the possible space, the second string indicates what characters are 
to the right, and the last string contains the number of spaces to insert. Note that the left string is in reverse order.
\par Each "string" is a quote, followed by a series of characters, ended by a quote, just like a C/C++ string. A backslash followed by anot
her backslash is interpreted as just a backslash, while a backslash followed by another character is interpreted as a control sequence. A capital "A" represents any non-space character.
\par The commands on the lines can be:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\{<number>\} and \{!<number>\} -- Indicate that the rule is to be used based upon the state of an options switch. For example, "\{5\}" indicates that the rule is used if switch 5 is on, while \{!5\}
 indicates the rule is only used if switch 5 is turned off. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {\{
E<one character in quotes>\} and \{O<one character in quotes>\}
 -- Indicates that the rules is turned on if there are an even (or odd) number of the specified character in the current line. A line is denoted by a carriage-return or line-feed character. Example: \{E"\\""\} looks for an even number of q
uotes in the current line. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here are subset of the spacing rules used for Voice Dictation English:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1  [Space]
\par  "" "." ""
\par  "" "," ""
\par  "" "\\\\" ""
\par  "" ":" ""
\par  "" ";" ""
\par  "" "%" ""
\par  "" "?" ""
\par  "" "!" ""
\par  "\\" " "" ""
\par  "" "\\"" " " \{E"\\""\}
\par  "" "\\"" ""  \{O"\\""\}
\par  "* " "" ""
