\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Since the rules are evaluated from first to last, the first rule evaluated is:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1  "" "." ""}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The first st
ring, containing empty quotes, means that it doesn't matter what text is to the left. The next string with just a period indicates that the rule is used if a period is to the right of the possible space insertion. The third string, empty quotes, means tha
t no text is inserted. Hence, the first rule says, don't insert spaces if there's a period to the right of it. This causes periods to "snuggle" up to text to the left.
\par Further on down the list you see:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1  .\\period "" "  " \{1\}
\par  .\\period "" " "  \{!1\}
\par  "?" "" "  " \{1\}
\par  "?" "" " " \{!1\}
\par  "!" "" "  " \{1\}
\par  "!" "" " " \{!1\}}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
This set of rules either places one or two spaces after sentences. If option switch 1 is TRUE then two spaces are inserted between sentences. If it's FALSE then only one space appears between senten
ces. The first string indicates that a sentence ends with a period, question mark, or exclamation point. The second string indicates that it doesn't matter what's to the right. The third string has one or twos space in it.
\par Another interesting set of rules is:
