\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Each line can be one of the following:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{A comment, starting with two slashes, "//". 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
The definition of a rule. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A rule definition is:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <rule name> = \{series of symbols\}}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The rule name can not have a space in it. It is cases insensitive. If the name is new then a new rule is created. If the name is a duplicate of another rule, then it as added as an alternative to the other rule. The main rule is <Start>, so every ru
le must be referenced directly or indirectly by <Start> for it to be used.
\par The symbols can be selected from the following:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{<rule name> -- Another rule. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
"string" -- A string that is entered into the text if the rule proves valid. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
word -- A word that must be spoken for the rule to be valid. 
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li1080\ri1080\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\cf1 Note}{\cf1 :\~\~\~For a compound or hyphenated word, you must remove all spaces (e.g., use twentyone or twenty-one, not twenty - one). 

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{[opt] -- Indicates that the next symbol is optional. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
[1+] -- Indicates that the next symbol must be repeated one or more times. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {\{<number>\}
 -- Requires an option switch to be TRUE in order for the rule to be valid. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {\{!<number>\}
 -- Requires an option switch to be FALSE in order for the rule to be valid. 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here some example rules:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <Start> = \{2\} <Acronym>
\par <Start> = \{3\} <Acronym>
\par <Acronym> = <Letter> [1+] <Letter>
\par <Letter> = A. "A" <PeriodAfter>
\par <Letter> = B. "B" <PeriodAfter>
\par <Letter> = C. "C" <PeriodAfter>
\par <Letter> = D. "D" <PeriodAfter>
\par // etc.
\par <PeriodAfter> = \{4\} "."
\par <PeriodAfter> = \{!4\} ""
\par <Acronym> = <Digit> [1+] <Digit>
\par <Digit> = zero "0"
\par <Digit> = oh "0"
\par <Digit> = one "1"
\par <Digit> = two "2"
\par // etc.}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The <Start> rule links all of the other rules. It allows a user to speak acronyms or digits. The \{2\} and \{3\}
 are option switches that allow the user to turn on/off the acronym and digit inverse text normalization.
\par The acronym rule requires that the user speaks at least two letters in a row before it turns on so that if the user dictates "fred z smith" it becomes "Fred Z. Smith" and not "Fred Z Smith". T
he <Acronym> rule references the <Letter> rule, which contains one entry per letter.
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <Letter> = B. "B" <PeriodAfter>}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The "B." indicates that the user speaks that letter. The ""B"" is the text typed in. The <PeriodAfter> rule might insert a period if rule 4
 is turned on, or nothing if it is off. If rule 4 is on, the dictated text, "F. E. I. F." becomes "F.E.I.F.", but if it's off, the dictated text becomes "FEIF".
\par Digits work in a similar manner.
