\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The call is asynchronous and returns right away. The application will be notified through a notification sink, }{\b ITelControlNotifySink}{
, when the control finishes. This allows an application to do other processing while the control is speaking and waiting for the user's response. If an app
lication wishes to make a synchronous call, it can use some of the sample code to turn the asynchronous call into a synchronous one, or it can use the C++ wrapper. (The C++ wrapper is described in a different section.)
\par When the control returns the recognition result to the application, the application will need to control the program flow. Depending upon the result it may access some databases, pull up another control, or hang up. It's up to the application.
\par Each control will have its own set of returns. Th
e yes/no control will return a value indicating if yes or no was spoken. Controls will also have error/exception returns that the application must handle. Most controls will have additional return values to indicate:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{The user hung up. 
