The "[<MyGrammar>]" rule must be filled in. Use the same format as specified in the Grammar Text File Format section. The developer should not replace the "[<Start>]" de
fault portion of the grammar, because this will preclude the built in commands, such as "What can I say?" from working. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {
The "[DTMFMap]" section contains a mapping of DTMF keys to recognition results. The DTMFMap section contains a series of values, with t
he DTMF symbol on the left, and the ItelControlNotifySink::Finish dwParam, on the right. (There's no way to specify the pMem parameter.) Example: "1=52" or "#=16". If an application does not specify the DTMFMap section the DTMF is not accepted. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s65 \f3\fs20\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s65\fi-300\li300\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\tx300{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls75\adjustright {Telephony 
grammars can also have a section, [DTMFString], which lets applications map continuous touch tones to a string. Under the DTMFString section, you must have the following: a Count field, which says how many digits to get, and then a character mapping for e
ach key. For example, to get a 4-digit number, the section might read: 
