Provides detailed information about a speech recognition event. The speech recognition results object is optional and may or may not be provided by the engine vendor.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Created by}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
If an engine supports speech recognition results objects, it creates a speech recognition results object each time a phrase is recognized. The engine returns the address of the }{\b IUnknown}{ interface on the results object with the corresponding }{\b 
ISRGramNotifySink}{::PhraseFinish or }{\b ISRGramNotifySink}{::PhraseHypothesis notification for the recognized phrase. 
\par The engine doesn't fully create the results object until the application calls an interface on the object. If the application ignores the not
ification, the engine releases the results object automatically. If the application calls an interface on the results object -- for example, }{\b IUnknown}{
::QueryInterface -- the engine finishes creating the results object and it is up to the application to release it.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Interacts with}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
An application can query a speech recognition results object to get detailed information about a recognition. The information that is available depends on the engine, but it may include a digital-audio recording, features extracted f
rom the audio stream, the most likely phrases recognized, and the name of the speaker.
\par An application can also use a results object to perform certain tasks, such as training the engine to a particular speaker's voice or reevaluating a recognition result. 
\par The information in a results object can occupy a significant amount of memory, so an application may instruct the object to discard certain parts of its data. In addition, an engine vendor may permit the results object to "deteriorate" or discard data aut
omatically as time passes. An application can preserve speech recognition results objects by locking them, or it can store results objects by archiving them with the associated grammar.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Interfaces}{
