common interface to the speech recognition and text-to-speech engines. It is also supported by the Voice Dictation object. It allows applications to set/get various "attributes" of an object. Calls into Voice Dictation are passed down to the engine, if th
e engine supports it.
\par Each attribute is identified by a unique DWORD value. System attributes (those defined in SAPI) range from 0x0001 to 0xffff. Attributes that are vendor-specific have a unique hi-word value, defined by two ANSI characters that uniquely 
identify the engine vendor. For example, Microsoft would have attributes of MAKELONG (x, MAKEWORD ('M', 'S')), where x ranges from 0x0001 to 0xffff.
\par An attribute can either be a DWORD, string, or memory address. The specific type will be defined by the attribute specification.
\par When an attribute is changed, the application is notified through }{\b ISRNotifySink}{::}{\b AttribChanged}{, }{\b ITTSNotifySink}{::}{\b AttribChanged}{, or }{\b IVDctNotifySink}{::}{\b AttribChanged}{. The }{\i dwAttrib}{
