\widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {szWord \cell Null-terminated string of variable size containing alphabetic characters and intra-word punctuation. The null at the end of the string is suffici
ent to indicate the end of the string. \line \line 
In most European languages, szWord is a string such as "hello" or "car." The text should not contain pronounced symbols. For example "345" is not a valid word string. The text also should not have ambiguous pronunci
ation. For example, instead of the string "IEEE," use three words: "I" "triple" "E". \line \line 
Certain languages such as Japanese use two character sets: a phonemic set and a symbolic set. The phonemic character set should be passed in szWord. If an application is
 trying to associate the symbolic representation of the word, it should use dwWordNum to point to the symbolic word. \line \line Applications may pass down words in the form of "text\\
pronunciation", where "text" is the text that's usually displayed and "pronunciation" is text that clarifies the pronunciation. For example "bye\\hello" would be pronounced as "hello", but would return the text, "bye\\
hello". In English, this convention is used for dictation words such as ".\\period" which has different semantics than "period", even though it has the same pronunciation. If an application passes the "word", ".\\
period" into a grammar, the recognizer should also return the string ".\\period" when the word is recognized.) \line \line If the dwFeatures flag of SRMODEINFO contains SRFEATURE_
IPAWORD and the pronunciation part of a SRWORDW begins with a "#" (pound-sign), then the text after represents IPA pronunciations. For example, "ha\\#h'a" is IPA. Applications can use this ability to specify custom pronunciations for CFGs or words. \line 
\line In Japanese, words will frequently be passed in as "kana\\
kanji," reducing the alternative pronunciations and increasing the accuracy. However, applications may just pass in "kanji," without the kana, in which case the engine should listen for all acceptable pron
unciations for the Kanji. Applications may also pass in just Kana, "Kana". The only place for which Japanese engines can reasonably expect to be given "help" is in the pronunciation of proper names. (Note: Japanese is reversed from other languages for bac
kwards compatibility.) \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
