\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Standard Grammar Types}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A grammar defines the words or phrases that an application c
an recognize. Speech recognition is based on grammars. This section describes the standard grammar types used.
\par An application can perform speech recognition by using three different types of grammars: context-free, dictation, and limited domain. Each type of grammar uses a different strategy for narrowing the set of sentences it will recognize:
\par Context-free grammar \line Uses rules that predict the next words that might possibly follow the word just spoken, reducing the number of candidates to evaluate in order to recognize the next word. 
\par Dictation grammar \line Defines a context for the speaker by identifying the subject of the dictation, the expected style of language, and what dictation has already been done. 
\par Limited-domain grammar \line Does not provide strict syntax structures, but does provide a set of words to recognize. Limited-domain grammar is a hybrid between a context-free grammar and a full dictation grammar. 
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 DirectSpeechRecognition API }{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Context-Free Grammars}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To
 reduce failed recognitions and computation, current speech recognition technology requires a list of valid sentences that can be spoken. However, such a list could include thousands or even a million entries. A context-free grammar scheme allows an engin
e to reduce the possible entries to a list of several hundred rules.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Examples}Examples of Typical Rules}{{\*\bkmkend Examples}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The following examples show typical rules that might be defined for a context-free grammar. 
\par The rule syntax shown here is for purposes of illustration only, and is not actually understood by the engine. The syntax for rules is up to the grammar designer.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sb120\sa120\sl-400\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f17\fs36\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Rules That Specify Lists}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A rule might specify a list of names such as "Bob," "Fred," "George," and "Bill," allowing the speaker to say only one of
 those names. Such a rule might appear as follows:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <Names>=ALT("Bob", "Fred", "George")}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In this example, ALT indicates that only one of the words can be spoken.
\par If the names are longer than one word, the rule might take either of the following forms:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <Names>=ALT("Bob Marks", "Fred Smith", "George Hill")}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {or
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <Names>=ALT(SEQ("Bob", "Marks"), SEQ("Fred", "Smith"), ...)}{\cf1 

\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In this example, SEQ indicates a sequence of words to be spoken.
\par As an alternative, the last name might be optional, in which case the rule might take the following form:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <Names>=ALT(SEQ("Bob",OPT("Marks")), ... }{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In this example, OPT indicates that the word can be spoken but is not required. This is equivalent to:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <Names>=ALT("Bob", "Bob Marks", "Fred", "Fred Smith", ...)}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sb120\sa120\sl-400\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f17\fs36\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Rules That Refer to Lists}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
A list of names is insufficient by itself for most recognition, but the list can be used as a component of another rule. For example, if several different actions -- such as "Send mail to <Name>" or "Who is <Name>" -- could be performed on a specific
 name, a rule might include alternatives and sequences that refer to the <Names> rule. For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <Actions>=ALT(SEQ("Send mail to",<Name>), SEQ("Who is", <Name>)), ...)}{
\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This example is equivalent to the following:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <Actions>=ALT("Send mail to Bob", "Send mail to Bob Marks",
\par     "Send mail to Fred", "Send mail to Fred Smith", ...)}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
A repeat operator, which allows its contents to be repeated indefinitely, is also useful. Rules are recursive, so the restriction of a single operand for repeat is not significant. For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <MoreActions>=SEQ(<Actions>, OPT(REP(SEQ("and", <Names>))))}{\cf1 

\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This kind of rule allows the speaker to say such things as "Send mail to Fred" or "Send mail to Fred and Bob and Bill."
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sb120\sa120\sl-400\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f17\fs36\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Lists That Are Filled at Run Time}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A rule can also refer to a 
list that is filled at run time after the grammar has been loaded. Rather than send text strings to the engine, which is too slow, the grammar sends a list of structures filled with the equivalent operations and symbols. Although lists may require more pr
ocessing to recognize, they allow grammars to be modified dynamically for such things as filenames that change frequently and often depend upon context. For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 <Actions> = ALT(LIST(<Names>))}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {For example, if the <Names> list contains "Bob Marks" and "F
red Smith" at run time, the speaker can say "Send mail to Bob Marks" and "Send mail to Fred Smith."
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Rule}Rule Activation}{{\*\bkmkend Rule}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To begin using a context-free grammar to recognize speech, the application must first activate a specific rule in the grammar. This 
puts the rule at the starting node of the recognition. 
\par Continuing the examples described at the beginning of this section, if an application activates the <Actions> rule, the speaker can say "Send mail to Bob Marks." However, if the application activates 
the <Names> rule instead, the user can say "Bob Marks" but cannot say "Send mail to Bob Marks."
\par Note that a grammar can have several rules activated at one time. For example, if both <Actions> and <Names> are activated, the speaker can say "Send mail to Bob Marks" or "Bob Marks."
\par The context-free grammar format requires a list of all of the rules that can be activated (called exported rules). Grammar designers should keep this list to a minimum because the efficiency of the grammar -- both the processing re
quired and the accuracy of recognition -- can be affected by the number of exported rules.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Automatic}Automatic Linking}{{\*\bkmkend Automatic}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Some engines support automatic linking between grammars. When grammars are linked, one grammar indicates that it imports a rule from another grammar and identifies the rule name by a string. If an application loads another grammar that exports a rule of t
h
at name, the first grammar borrows that rule and all of the vocabulary referenced by the rule from the second grammar, even if the second grammar is not activated. In the case of a conflict, the first grammar links to the grammar that has been loaded the 
longest.
\par For example, a grammar that allows the speaker to say "Set the time to }{\cs80\v\cf6 <Time>}{" might list }{\cs80\v\cf6 <Time>}{as an imported rule. A second grammar might have a }{\cs80\v\cf6 <Time>}{rule that p
rovides the vocabulary and syntax for recognizing the time. As soon as the second grammar is loaded, the first grammar would automatically link its }{\cs80\v\cf6 <Time>}{rule to the second grammar's }{\cs80\v\cf6 <Time>}{rule.
\par Automatic linking allows third parties to develop improved or
 shared grammars for commonly used syntaxes, such as numbers, dates, and times. If a third party wishes to keep the grammar format secret, the grammar can be compiled into the custom formats of each engine that is supported, rather than shipping the gramm
ar in a standard format.
\par Automatic linking should be used with care because name conflicts can occur or grammars can change invisibly to the application. If an imported rule cannot be linked, the original grammar should still work even though portions of it
 may be inaccessible. In addition, IDs of words that originate from different grammars will be invalid because the application won't be able to identify which grammar provided the word.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Run}Run-Time Lists}{{\*\bkmkend Run}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Some engines support run-time lists, which allow 
a grammar to indicate a list that will be supplied at run time. This provides fill-in-the-blank functionality for a grammar for information such as e-mail names, filenames, fonts, and other values that cannot be predicted when the grammar is compiled. 

\par The syntax for using a run-time list is the same as for words as rules. If a list is not filled in at run time, the grammar should still work even though portions of it may be inaccessible.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Recursion}Recursion and Perplexity}{{\*\bkmkend Recursion}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Context-free grammars are 
very powerful tools because of their recursion ability, but they can also be made overly complex. The efficiency of a speech recognition engine deteriorates when using grammar with too much recursion (more than several levels) or too much perplexity, whic
h is essentially the number of possible choices at a given node in a recognition path.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Information}Information Chunks in Context-Free Grammars}{
{\*\bkmkend Information}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The context-free grammar follows the general grammar format described in the section "General Format of 
a Grammar," earlier in this section. A context-free grammar contains one (and only one) of each of the following information chunks:
\par }\trowd \trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf11 \cltxlrtb \cellx2947\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf11 \cltxlrtb 
