(required) This chunk contains a list of SRCFGXRULE structures that specify the exported rules for the grammar. When an application activates a context-free grammar, it must specify which exported rule to activate. If the engine supports automatic linking
, this is a list of rules to link. A grammar must have at least one exported rule. Many engines lose efficiency when using too many exported rules, so keep exported rules to a minimum. Rule names are case insensitive.\cell }\pard\plain 
\widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Imported Rules (SRCKCFG_IMPORTRULES)\cell (
optional) This chunk contains a list of SRCFGIMPRULE structures that specify the imported rules for the grammar. Imported rules are automatically linked to exported rules as soon as the application loads another grammar that has the same rule name as an i
mported rule. Some engines do not support automatic linking and cannot load grammars with imported rules. Rule names are case insensitive.\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 
\row }\trowd \trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \cltxlrtb \cellx2947\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \cltxlrtb \cellx7320\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0
\widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Run-Time Lists (SRCKCFG_LISTS)\cell (optional) This chunk contains a list of SRCFGLIST structures that specify the run-tim
e lists that the grammar requires. List names are case insensitive.\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0
\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 DirectSpeechRecognition API }{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Dictation Grammars}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A dictation grammar defines a context in which to speak, which loosely identifies the subje
ct of the dictation, the kind of language style that is expected, and what dictation has been done in the past. 
\par A dictation grammar does not contain any information about the language model (the engine has that information) nor does it specify all of the words that can be spoken. It specifies only unusual words or expected groups of words. 
