\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
DirectTextToSpeech is a low-level interface for implementing text-to-speech. This reference describes: a simple coding example, DirectTextToSpeech COM objects, interfaces, data structures, error values, and control tags.
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 DirectTextToSpeech API }{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Introduction To Using The DirectTextToSpeech API}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This section demonstrates how to perform the following tasks: 
\par Initialize an application for using text-to-speech translation. 
\par Add a text buffer to an engine's speaking queue. 
\par Process buffer-related notifications. 
\par Initializing an application for text-to-speech includes creating the multimedia audio-destination object, the text-to-speech enumerator, the engine object, and the application's buffer-notification object.
\par For more coding examples, see the sample applications provided with the SDK.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Initializing an Application for Text-To-Speech}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To implement text-to-speech features, you must initialize the OLE libraries and then create instances of the audio-destination obj
ect and the text-to-speech engine object. The engine object translates a text buffer into audio output and passes it to the audio-destination object, which plays the speech output on the audio-output device. In addition, you can use the text-to-speech enu
merator and engine enumerator to find an engine and mode that meets your application's requirements. 
\par The following example demonstrates how to initialize an application for text-to-speech. It shows how to create the necessary objects for implementing text-to-speech translation. The example accomplishes the following tasks:
\par Uses the }{\b CoInitialize}{ function to initialize the OLE libraries. 
\par Creates an instance of the multimedia audio-destination object, which allows the engine to send speech output to the multimedia wave-out driver. 
\par Uses the text-to-speech enumerator to find an engine and mode that most closely matches the application's requirements. 
\par Creates an instance of an application-defined class that implements the }{\b ITTSBufNotifySink}{ interface. The engine calls this interface to notify the application of buffer-related events. 
\par The function creates a multimedia audio-destination object, finds and selects a text-to-speech engine and mode, and creates the buffer notification object. It returns TRUE if successful or FALSE if an error occurs. It uses the following global variables:

\par g_pIAMM -- address of IAudioMultiMediaDevice interface 
\par g_TTSModeInfo -- address of }{\b TTSMODEINFO}{ structure that contains information about the selected mode 
\par g_pIBufNotifySink -- address of the application's }{\b ITTSBufNotifySink}{ interface 
\par }\trowd \trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf11 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf11 \cltxlrtb \cellx7320\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\intbl
\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\field{\*\fldinst {PRIVATE}{{\*\datafield 
020203001a0200000000017e0000000000000000000001000000000000000000cf1f010000001a0000000100000000000000000000000000000001000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000003c6cb0585c6cb051b000000adc6cb051c000000cf1f0000000000008c00000000000000000000000000
00000000000000000000000000000000}}}{\fldrslt }}{\cs82 
\par BOOL FindAndSelectEngine(void)
\par \{
\par     HRESULT hRes;          // result code
\par     TTSMODEINFO ttsmiTemp; // information about the desired mode
\par     PITTSFIND pITTSFind;   // address of ITTSFind interface
\par     // Initialize OLE.
\par     if (FAILED(CoInitialize(NULL)))
\par         return FALSE;
\par     // Create the multimedia audio-destination object and retrieve the
\par     // address of the object's IAudioMultiMediaDevice interface.
\par     hRes = CoCreateInstance(CLSID_MMAudioDest, NULL, CLSCTX_ALL,
\par         IID_IAudioMultiMediaDevice, (void**) &g_pIAMM);
\par     if (hRes)
\par         return FALSE;
\par 
\par     // Set the device identifier for the multimedia wave-out device.
\par     g_pIAMM-DeviceNumSet(WAVE_MAPPER);
\par     // Create the text-to-speech enumerator and retrieve the address of
\par     // the object's ITTSFind interface.
\par     hRes = CoCreateInstance(CLSID_TTSEnumerator, NULL, CLSCTX_ALL,
\par         IID_ITTSFind, (void**) &pITTSFind);
\par     if (FAILED(hRes)) \{
\par         g_pIAMM-Release;
\par         return FALSE;
\par     \}
\par     // Define the characteristics of the desired text-to-speech mode.
\par     ZeroMemory(&ttsmiTemp, sizeof(ttsmiTemp));
\par     ttsmiTemp.language.LanguageID =
\par         MAKELANGID(LANG_ENGLISH, SUBLANG_ENGLISH_US);
\par     ttsmiTemp.wGender = GENDER_FEMALE;
\par     ttsmiTemp.wAge = TTSAGE_ADULT;
\par     lstrcpy(ttsmiTemp.szStyle, "Business");
\par     // Find the engine and mode that most closely matches the desired
\par     // characteristics.
\par     hRes = pITTSFind-Find(&ttsmiTemp, NULL, &g_TTSModeInfo);
\par     if (hRes) \{
\par         g_pIAMM-Release;
\par         pITTSFind-Release();
\par         return FALSE;
\par     \}
\par     // Select the text-to-speech mode and create an engine object to
\par     // represent the mode. Also, retrieve the address of the engine's
\par     // ITTSCentral interface.
\par     hRes = pITTSFind-Select(g_TTSModeInfo.gModeID, &g_pITTSCentral,
\par         (LPUNKNOWN) g_pIAMM);
\par     if (hRes) \{
\par         g_pIAMM-Release;
\par         pITTSFind-Release();
\par         return FALSE;
\par     \};
\par     // Release the text-to-speech enumerator; it is no longer needed.
\par     pITTSFind-Release();
\par     // Create an instance of the buffer notification object based on the
\par     // application-defined CBufNotify class.
\par     if ((g_pIBufNotifySink = new CBufNotify) == NULL)
\par         return FALSE;
\par     return TRUE;
\par \}}{\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Adding a Text Buffer to the Speaking Queue}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An application causes a text-to-speech engine to speak text by placing text buffers in the engine's speaki
ng queue. To place a buffer in the queue, an application fills an }{\b SDATA}{ structure with the address of the text buffer and the amount of text in the buffer. The application passes the buffer to the engine by calling the }{\b ITTSCentral}{::}{\b 
TextData}{ member function. When calling }{\b TextData}{, the application also specifies:
\par The character set that the engine should use to translate the text in the buffer, 
\par A flag that indicates whether the buffer contains control tags 
\par (optionally) The address of the application's }{\b ITTSBufNotifySink}{ interface. The engine uses this interface to notify the application of buffer-related events. 
\par The following example shows how to add a text buffer to the speaking queue.
\par The function puts a text buffer into the text-to-speech engine's speaking queue and returns TRUE if successful or FALSE if an error occurs. }{\i pszTextBuffer}{ is address of the text buffer. The function uses the following global variable: }{\i 
g_pIBufNotifySink}{ -- address of the }{\b ITTSBufNotifySink}{ interface.
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\intbl\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\field{\*\fldinst {PRIVATE}{{\*\datafield 
020203001a0200000000017e0000000000000000000001000000000000000000cf1f010000001a0100000100000000000000000000000000000001000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000019cecb053ccecb051b0100005acecb051c010000cf1f0000000000008c00000000000000000000000000
00000000000000000000000000000000}}}{\fldrslt }}{\cs82 
\par BOOL SpeakTheText(LPSTR pszTextBuffer)
\par \{
\par     HRESULT hRes;
\par     SDATA data;
\par 
\par      data.dwSize = lstrlen(pszTextBuffer);
\par      data.pData = pszTextBuffer;
\par     // Put the text buffer into the engine's speaking queue, and give
\par     // the engine the address of the ITTSBufNotifySink interface for
\par     // buffer-related notifications.
\par     hRes = g_pITTSCentral-TextData(CHARSET_TEXT, 0, data,
\par         g_pIBufNotifySink, IID_ITTSBufNotifySink);
\par     if (FAILED(hRes))
\par         return FALSE;
\par     return TRUE;
\par \}}{\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Processing Buffer Notifications}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The text-to-speech engine object calls an application's }{\b ITTSBufNotifySink}{
 interface to inform the application of buffer-related events so that the application can respond to them. To receive notifications, the application must create a COM object that supports the }{\b ITTSBufNotifySink}{
 interface and must pass the address of the interface to the engine object when calling the }{\b ITTSCentral}{::}{\b TextData}{ member function. 
\par The }{\b ITTSBufNotifySink}{ interface consists of a set of member functions that correspond to buffer-related events. When an event occurs, the engine object calls the member function that corresponds to the event. 
\par The following example shows how to define an object class that implements the }{\b ITTSBufNotifySink}{ interface.
