\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Represents a text-to-speech mode of an engine, which processes text into audible speech. An engine typically provides an assortment of text-to-speec
h modes that can be used to play text in different voices or audio sampling rates. The engine object is supplied by the engine vendor.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Created by}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To create an engine object, you first use a text-to-speech enumerator to locate the mode to use and then call the }{\b ITTSEnum}{
::Select member function with the GUID of the mode and the address of the }{\b IUnknown}{ interface of an audio-destination object.
\par As the engine object initializes, it calls the }{\b IAudio}{ interface on the audio-destination object to determine whether or not it can use that audio destination. If it can use it, the engine object calls the }{\b IAudio}{
::PassNotify member function with an }{\b IAudioDestNotifySink}{ notification interface, so the audio-destination object can notify the engine when audio data is played. I
f the engine cannot use the specified audio destination, the Select member function returns an error and the engine object releases itself.
\par If Select is successful, it returns the address of the }{\b ITTSCentral}{ interface on the newly created engine object.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Interacts with}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The engine object receives notifications from the audio-destination object about the status of audio being played, such as whether the audio-destination object has space available for more text.
\par The engine object can notify the application about events that pertain to text-to-speech, such the time that audio starts or stops playing. To receive notifications, the application calls the }{\b ITTSCentral}{
::Register member function on the engine object and passes the address of the notification interface. The application must create a COM object that supports the }{\b ITTSNotifySink}{
 interface. The application developer determines how the application responds to notifications.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Interfaces}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The engine object should support the }{\b ITTSAttributes}{, }{\b ITTSCentral}{, and }{\b ITTSDialogs}{
 interfaces, and it may also support the }{\b ILexPronounce}{ interface. An engine object may support other interfaces defined by the engine vendor to provide additional functionality.
\par There are the following interfaces.
