IAttributes is a common interface to the speech recognition and text-to-speech engines. It is also supported by the Voice Dictation object. 
It allows applications to set/get various "attributes" of an object. Calls into Voice Dictation are passed down to the engine, if the engine supports it.
\par Each attribute is identified by a unique DWORD value. System attributes (those defined in SAPI) range 
from 0x0001 to 0xffff. Attributes that are vendor-specific have a unique hi-word value, defined by two ANSI characters that uniquely identify the engine vendor. For example, Microsoft would have attributes of MAKELONG (x, MAKEWORD ('M', 'S')), where x ran
ges from 0x0001 to 0xffff.
\par An attribute can either be a DWORD, string, or memory address. The specific type will be defined by the attribute specification.
\par When an attribute is changed, the application is notified through }{\b ISRNotifySink}{::}{\b AttribChanged}{, }{\b ITTSNotifySink}{::}{\b AttribChanged}{, or }{\b IVDctNotifySink}{::}{\b AttribChanged}{. The }{\i dwAttrib}{
 parameter values returned in the notification are the same as those listed in the table below.
\par Error values:
\par E_ACCESSDENIED 
\par E_BUFFERTOOSMALL 
\par E_INVALIDARG 
\par E_NOTIMPL 
\par E_OUTOFMEMORY 
\par E_UNEXPECTED 
\par E_WRONGTYPE 
\par The following interfaces are supported:
\par DWORDGet 
\par DWORDSet 
\par MemoryGet 
\par MemorySet 
\par StringGet 
\par StringSet 
\par For a text-to-speech engine, the }{\i dwAttrib}{ parameter can be one of the following values:
