\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The ILexPronounce interface allows a user to correct words in the engine's pronunciation lexicon. The ILexProno
unce interface is supported by a speech recognition engine and a text-to-speech engine. It has these member functions:
\par Add 
\par Get 
\par Remove 
\par For a }{\i text-to-speech engine}{, the database of pronunciations includes the user exception dictionary and the letter-to-sound rules. The only reason a pronunciation wouldn't exist is if a word were unpronounceable.
\par For a }{\i speech recognition engine}{, the database affects the recognizer's backup lexicon, which is used for generating context-free grammars. This will not affect dic
tation recognition because it doesn't specify which dictation language model is affected.
\par ILexPronounce may also be used by other objects in the system, such as a speech recognition grammar object.
\par For a }{\i speech recognition grammar object}{ (dictation and limited domain grammars only), the database affects the "topic" used in the grammar. In some engines, changing a pronunciation in a topic may affect the pronunciations of other topics.
\par If a speech recognition engine supports ILexPronounce, it also supports th
e International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA) character set. Engines need to support ILexPronounce with IPA as a means of transferring pronunciations between the application and the engine. If an application wishes to display the pronunciation to the end user, 
i
t can (but doesn't necessarily have to) use the Speech Tool's Phoneme Converter object to convert between IPA and other phoneme representations. The Phoneme Converter defaults to Comlex for English, and as new resources are added, will default to other la
nguage specific phoneme representations.
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\cf1 For a more detailed description of Comlex}{\cf1 \~\~\~please refer to http://www.ldc.upenn.edu/readme_files/comlex.readme.html.
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Pronunciations are Windows-login dependent. If a new user logs onto the system using the Windows 9
5 or NT log-on screen, the pronunciation exceptions change. They are both engine-dependent and language-dependent, so pronunciations are not shared between languages, or different engines, but may be shared between modes of the same engine. 
