TextData is asynchronous -- that is, the member function returns before all of the text is played.
\par TextData passes the text to the engine, which performs the actual conversion of text to audio; however, this function may initiate the conversion in a different thread. The engine can use a background thread to convert the text.
\par Text is loaded into a series of buffers. As each buffer is loaded, the engine converts the text to audio. The engine treats the end of each buffer as an end of data and does not try to merge the data streams together.
\par For best results, the }{\b\cf1 SDATA}{\cf1  structure specified by }{\i\cf1 dText}{\cf1  should contain the address of a complete sentence or paragraph. If the structure contains the address of
 a complete sentence or paragraph, the text-to-speech buffer that }{\b\cf1 TextData}{\cf1  passes to the engine for conversation to audio data will be aligned on a sentence or paragraph boundary.
\par Initially, an engine object plays any text that is sent to it as soon as pos
sible. To change this state, you should call the ITTSCentral::AudioPause member function. Once AudioPause is called, no audio is output until the ITTSCentral::AudioResume member function is called.
\par Because of the ability for latent error returns, engines should avoid using }{\b\cf1 ITTSNotifySink2}{\cf1 ::}{\b\cf1 Error}{\cf1  as much as possible, since most applications will not use the notification sink or will ignore the results. This means that 
}{\b\cf1 TextData}{\cf1  should still report an error if it can't claim the wave device, and that if an error occurs sometime during speaking, that the engine should send the normal }{\b\cf1 ITTSNotifySink}{\cf1 ::AudioStop and }{\b\cf1 ITTSBufNotifySink}
{\cf1 ::TextDataDone messages.
