h one to use, it calls Select to create the engine object.
\par As the engine object initializes, it calls the }{\b\cf1 IAudio}{\cf1  interface of the audio-destination object specified by pIUnknownForAudio to determine whether it can use that audio destination. If it can, the function returns an }{\b\cf1 ITTSCentral}
{\cf1  interface for the newly created engine object. If it cannot, the engine object releases itself and the Select function returns an error.
\par After the object is created, it calls the }{\b\cf1 IAudio}{\cf1 ::PassNotify member function to pass i
ts own notification sink to the audio-destination object. Passing the notification sink to the audio-destination object allows the object to notify the engine when audio data is played. When all references to the engine object are released, the engine obj
ect calls the }{\b\cf1 IAudio}{\cf1 ::Release member function.
\par The multimedia audio-destination object can be used to send audio to a wave-out device. The Select function passes the }{\b\cf1 IUnknown}{\cf1  interface rather than the }{\b\cf1 IAudioDest}{\cf1 
 interface because, although all engines should support IAudioDest, some engines may use custom interfaces for dealing with special hardware or drivers and }{\b\cf1 IUnknown}{\cf1  can be used to obtain such interfaces.
