\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
This section describes the text-to-speech control tags that can be embedded in the source text to improve the prosody of text-to-speech translation:
\par Syntax 
\par Conventions 
\par Chr 
\par Com 
\par Ctx (some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par Dem (new for SAPI 4.0 -- some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par Emp (some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par Eng 
\par Mrk 
\par Pau 
\par Pit 
\par Pra (new for SAPI 4.0 -- some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par Prn (some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par Pro (some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par Prt (some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par RmS (new for SAPI 4.0 -- some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par RmW (new for SAPI 4.0 -- some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par RPit (new for SAPI 4.0 -- some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par RPrn (new for SAPI 4.0 -- some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par RSpd (new for SAPI 4.0 -- some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par Rst (new for SAPI 4.0) 
\par Spd 
\par Vce (some speech engines may not support this tag) 
\par Vol 
\par A text-to-speech engine can usually translate individual words to speech successfully. However, as soon as the engine speaks a sentence, the perceived quality of i
ts translation decreases because the engine cannot correctly synthesize human prosody -- the inflection, accent, and timing of human speech.
\par The prosody of translated speech can be improved by using text-to-speech control tags to better simulate human speech. Control tags can be embedded in the source text passed to an engine with the }{\b ITTSCentral}{
::TextData member function, or they can be inserted into the text that is currently playing by calling the }{\b ITTSCentral}{::Inject member function. This allows an appli
cation to alter prosody of text as it is spoken, without having to reconstruct the text-to-speech buffers. 
\par This section describes control tags that you can use to alter the prosody of text translated into speech. All tags are optional except the bookmark (Mrk) tag, which must be supported. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Syntax}Syntax}{{\*\bkmkend Syntax}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Text-to-speech control tags follow these general rules of syntax:
\par All tags begin and end with a backslash character (\\). 
\par The backslash character is not allowed within a tag. 
\par An odd number of backslash characters in tagged text produce undefined behavior in the engine. 
\par Tags are case-insensitive. For example, }{\cs82\f2 \\vce\\}{ is the same as }{\cs82\f2 \\VCE\\}{. 
\par Tags are white-space -- dependent. For example, }{\cs82\f2 \\Rst\\}{ is not the same as }{\cs82\f2 \\ Rst \\}{. 
\par To include a backslash character in tagged text, but outside a tag, use a double backslash (\\\\).
\par If the application has the tagged text bit on and wishes to speak a filename, such as "c:\\windows\\system\\test.txt", then it should double up the backslashes (e.g., "c:\\\\windows\\\\system\\\\test.txt").
\par Samples:
\par }{\cs82\f2 \\ ctx="e-mail" \\}{ - Should be parsed as an "e-mail" tag. 
\par }{\cs82\f2 \\ctx="e-mail"}{ - Should be ignored since it's an unclosed tag at the end of a document. 
\par }{\cs82\f2 \\\\ctx="e-mail"\\\\}{ - Speaks "back-slash c t x equals e-mail back-slash" 
\par \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ctx="e-mail"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ - Speaks "backslash backslash backslash backslash c t x equals e-mail backslash backslash backslash backslash" 
\par }{\cs82\f2 \\ctm="e-mail"\\}{ - Ignored because it's an unknown tag. 
\par When the text-to-speech engine encounters a tag it does not understand, the tag is ignored. 
\par Tags are persistent from one call to the }{\b ITTSCentral}{::TextData member function to another. For example, if the }{\cs82\f2 \\ctx="e-mail"\\}{
 tag is passed to an engine that supports that tag, the engine stays in the "e-mail" context until another tag changes the context.
