\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
This section contains reference information about the interfaces and member functions that the audio objects support. Because the interfaces are intended to be called by an engine rather than an ap
plication, the information in this section is intended primarily for engine developers rather than application developers. This information is also intended for developers who need to write a custom audio object.
\par Application developers can write their own audio source or destination objects to rout the audio as they desire. Some typical uses might be:
\par Speech Recognition Over the Network. The audio source gets audio from a network pipe rather than a wave device. 
\par Echo, Chorus, and other Effects. The audio destination object does some digital effects on the audio before passing it to another audio destination object. 
\par Text-to-Speech Over the Network. Pipe the audio from the audio destination through a network pipe to another computer. 
\par Get notified ahead of time. If some of the timing notifications come right when the text-to-speech audio is being played, then the animation will always lag behind the audio. An application can write an audio destination object that passes all of the data
 to another audio destination, object except that it re-maps bookmarks so they happen a bit sooner than the text-to-speech engine thinks. 
\par Writing an audio source or destination object is straight forward. Look on the speech SDK for a sample.
\par This reference describes: the Audio COM objects, interfaces, and error values.
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Audio Objects API }{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 COM Objects}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The basic audio source/destination objects are:
\par DirectSound Audio Destination \line This audio destination object sends audio to the DirectSound APIs. Most games will use this for text-to-speech. 
\par DirectSound Audio Source \line This audio source object receives audio from the DirectSound 5.0 (or better) APIs. Most games will use this for speech recognition. 
\par File Audio Destination \line This audio destination object writes audio to a file. 
\par File Audio Source \line This audio source object reads the audio from a file. 
\par Multimedia Audio Destination \line This audio destination object sends audio to the multimedia APIs. Most applications will use this for text-to-speech. 
\par Multimedia Audio Source \line This audio source object receives audio from the multimedia APIs. Most applications will use this for speech recognition. 
\par Microsoft also provides a number of audio source/destination objects that insert themselves between the basic audio source/destination objects and the speech recognition or text to speech engines:
\par Instrumented Audio Source \line Writes the digital audio to a file, or replaces the incoming live digital audio with data coming from a recording. 
\par Logging Audio Source (new for SAPI 4.0) \line The logging audio source writes out logging information to the Speech Tools Logging Object. 
\par Suspending Audio Source \line This audio source allows an application to temporarily suspend input from a speech recognizer without having to deactivate all of the grammars. 
\par Telephony Audio Source (new for SAPI 4.0) \line Audio source pass-through that provides functionality that helps with telephony. The object will convert from mu/a-law if necessary, and allows the input audio s
ource to be changed from underneath the speech recognition engine. 
\par Telephony Audio Destination (new for SAPI 4.0) \line Audio destination pass-through that provides functionality that helps with telephony. The object will convert to mu/a-law if necessary, and 
allows the input audio source to be changed from underneath the speech recognition engine. 
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Audio Objects API }{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 DirectSound Audio Destination COM Object}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The DirectSound audio destination object sends the audio through a DirectSound object, rather than a multimedia wave-device or wave file.

\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Created by}{
