\par [(Commands)]
\par 1=open the window
\par 2=close the window
\par 3=set the time to "Time=" <time>
\par 4=set the date to "Date=" <date>}{\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The application then 
creates the grammar compiler object by calling CoCreateInstance (..., CLSID_STGramComp, ...), and passes the grammar's file into pISTGramComp->FromFile(). The application can also have the data loaded into memory, a resource, or an IStream, and call appro
priate functions such as pISTGramComp->FromMemory(). To compile the grammar into a standard grammar format understandable by speech recognition engines, the application calls pISTGramComp->Compile().
\par When the application is ready to start the grammar listen
ing, it must create a speech recognition engine using the DirectSpeechRecognition API. It can call pISTGramComp->GrammarDataGet() and pass the data into the engine's pISRCentral->GrammarLoad() call, or it can use a helper function in the grammar compiler.
 Calling pISTGramComp->GrammarLoad (}{\b pISRCentral}{, ...) will load the grammar and initialize any lists. The grammar will start recognizing as soon as ISRGramCommon->Activate() is called.
\par When a recognition is returned by PhraseFinish(), the application passes the }{\b PSRPHRASEW}{
 pointer into pISTGramComp->PhraseParse(). This parses the recognition and returns a command ID and parse string. The contents of the command ID and parse string are determined by the text description of the grammar. In the case of the simpl
e grammar listed above, if "set the time to five oh four p m" were recognized, the parsed command ID would be "4", and the string would be "Time=5:04 PM".
\par The SDK contains SRG.EXE, which demonstrates how to use the grammar compiler.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Logging}Logging Object}{{\*\bkmkend Logging}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {(New for SAPI 4.0)
\par Applications use the logging object to keep a record
 of what happened while the application was run. Several speech API components (such as the Telephony Information Object, Text-to-speech Queue, and Logging Audio Source) can be passed a logging object and will log any pertinent data. Application developer
s can use the logged information to improve their system.
\par The most common use for the logging object is a server telephony application. Developers of telephony server applications can collect huge amounts of data about their users such as how they respond t
o specific prompts and how successful they are navigating the system. The application designers can use then use the information to fine tune their applications and improve performance. 
\par A typical telephony application might use the logging object like so:
\par Create the logging object. If it's using the Telephony Information Object's easy initialization function then that can automatically create and connect the information object. If not, the application would create the logging object with a CoCreateInstance
 (..., CLSID_STLog, ...). 
\par Make sure that audio recordings are logged: 
\par The application can create a logging audio source, with CoCreateInstance (..., CLSID_AudioSourceLog, ...). 
\par Using pIAudioSourceLog->UseLogging() it passes the logging object to the audio source. It also passes the real audio source (connected to the multimedia device or direct sound) using pIAudioSourceLog->AudioSource(). 
\par The logging object is passed to the speech recognizer when pISRFind->Select() is called. 
\par An application can also have the spoken text-to-speech strings logged by passing the logging object to the Text-to-Speech Queue, pISTTTSQueue->UseLogging(). 
\par When a telephone call starts, the application generates a file name to write the log into and passes it into pISTLog->ToFil
e(). The application can also log the data to a stream. When the call is finished the application calls pISTLog->ToFile(NULL) to close the file. 
\par Already, the application will log lots of information, such as what the text-to-speech engine speaks, and recordings of everything the user says. 
\par If the application uses the telephony information object and Microsoft telephony controls even more information will be logged:
\par DTMF keys pressed. 
\par What the speech recognition engine actually recognized. 
\par When a control starts and stops. 
\par The responses that a user gives within a control. 
\par The application can even log some of its own application data by calling:
