\par <Pizza> = "Pizza" pizza [opt] pie
\par <Pizza> = "Pizza:" <Topping> pizza [opt] pie
\par <Pizza> = "Pizza:" <Topping> [1+] <AndTopping> pizza [opt] pie
\par <Topping> = pepperoni "Pepperoni"
\par <Topping> = sausage "Sausage"
\par <Topping> = onion "Onion"
\par <AndTopping> = [opt] and "+" <Topping>}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The rule <Pizza> can be seen by other rules. The rules for <Topping> and <AndTopping> are only available to the <Pizza> rule and cannot be seen or be affected by other rules. If there is another rule called <Topp
ing> then it's not used in the generation of <Pizza>.
\par Each rule line represents an alternative case that's valid. It begins with the rule name, followed by equals, followed by a number of operators. These operators are conditions that must be met to satisfy the rule. The operators can be:
