}\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {...\cell Wild card. Any number of words (zero or more) or any type can be spoken. Applications can use this for word spotting.\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright 
\fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {...2\cell A special wild card that has the following properties: 
\par If compiled into a context-free grammar (compileto=cfg), then the ...2 is ignored. 
\par Otherwise, during parsing (if compileto=limited-domain or compileto=dictation) it's treated just like the parsing of a "..." except that the word strings are added to the parse string, so the application will know what words were spoken. 
\par Applications can use this like so: 
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\intbl\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82 [Grammar]
\par Type=CFG
\par CompileTo=Dictation
\par 
\par [<Start>]
\par <Start> =my name is "Name=" ...2 }{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {For the input string of "My name is george smith", the parse will come out "Name=George Smith " \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row 
}\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {[opt]\cell The next symbol (word or rule) is optional and doesn't have to be spoken.\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\trowd 
