\fs20\cgrid {[0+] or [1+]\cell The next symbol (word or rule) can be repeated either zero or more times [0+], or one or more times [1+].\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \fs20\cf14\cgrid {\cf0 \row }\pard\plain 
\s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Command}Command Rules}{{\*\bkmkend Command}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Context-free grammars can contain sections that define "Command Rules." These sect
ions are identified by the rule name surrounded with "[(" and ")]". Each line of the section contains a valid command.
\par An example command rule is:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 [(OrderPizza)]
\par 1=I want <Pizza> delivered
\par 2=I want to pick up <Pizza>
\par 3=What can I say}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The number indicates the command number. It's either blank (defaulting to 0), decimal, or hexadecimal (using C notation with 0x in front). The command number returned by PhraseParse is the sum of all command numbers present in the parse. You can use the s
umming to make certain bits of the command number indicate meaning. For example, the high word might be a verb number, and the low word a noun number.
\par The command rule lines support the same operators as parse string rules.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Lists}Lists}{{\*\bkmkend Lists}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Context-free gram
mars can contain a [Lists] section that indicates what lists are to be exported in the CFG. The values of the list should contain a series of "=list name" entries. Whenever these are referenced by a rule (with angle brackets surrounding the names) they wi
ll be treated as lists instead of other rules.
\par A sample [Lists] section is:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 [Lists]
\par =PizzaTopping
\par =City}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A list can be used in a rule by putting the list name in angle brackets:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 [<Pizza>]
\par <Pizza> = "Pizza" pizza [opt] pie
\par <Pizza> = "Pizza:" <PizzaTopping> pizza [opt] pie
\par <Pizza> = "Pizza:" <PizzaTopping> [1+] <AndTopping> pizza [opt] pie
\par <AndTopping> = [opt] and "+" <PizzaTopping>}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When PhraseParse() is called, the string of the recognized word in the list will be placed on the parse string. Example: If the <P
izzaTopping> list contains "Pepperoni", then the recognized phrase, "pepperoni pizza" would result in the parse string, "Pizza:Pepperoni".
\par Applications that compile CFGs to limited-domain or dictation grammars can use lists. Since limited-domain and dictation grammar objects do not support }{\b ListSet}{
, the word that fits into the list slot is used. A grammar to ask the user's name using a "CompileTo=Limited-Domain" could be:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 [Grammar]
\par LangID=1033
\par Type=CFG
\par CompileTo=dictation
\par [<Start>]
\par <Start> = "First:" <FirstName>
\par <Start> = "First:" <FirstName> " Last:" <LastName>
\par [Lists]
\par =FirstName
\par =LastName}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Default}Default Lists}{{\*\bkmkend Default}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Context-free grammars that are compiled to CFG can have default list entries. Default list entries are automatically sent to the grammar object
 when pISRGramComp-GrammarLoad() or pISRGramComp-AutoList() are called. Default lists are defined by providing a section named after the list, and have one or more lines of "=list words".
\par A sample definition of default lists is:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 [Lists]
\par =PizzaTopping
\par =City
\par 
\par [PizzaTopping]
\par =Pepperoni
\par =Sardines
\par =Sausage
\par =Green Peppers
\par 
\par [City]
\par =Redmond
\par =Bellevue
\par =Seattle}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Topics}Topics}{{\*\bkmkend Topics}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Topics are supported by limited-domain and dictation grammars. The topic section is titled [Topics] and contains one or more topics that describe the grammar.
\par A topics section might look like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 [Topics]
\par =E-mail
\par =Computer}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Words}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The words section is supported by limited-domain and dictation grammars. It is titled [Words] and contains one or more words used in the grammar. For limited-domain
 grammars it lists the valid words, and for dictation it lists likely words.
\par A words section might look like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 [Words]
\par =aardvark
\par =antelope
\par =nyala
\par =kangaroo}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Word}Word Groups}{{\*\bkmkend Word}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The word groups section is supported by limited-domain and dictation grammars. The word groups section is titled [WordGroups] and contains one or more word group names used in the grammar. Each word group must also have a corresponding section with a list
 of words.
\par A word group section might look like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 [WordGroups]
\par =Animals
\par =Cities
\par 
\par [Animals]
\par 
\par =aardvark
\par =antelope
\par =nyala
\par =kangaroo
\par 
\par [Cities]
\par =Redmond
\par =Bellevue
\par =Seattle}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Frames}Frames}{{\*\bkmkend Frames}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A frame-based grammar is a more loosely defined grammar than a CFG,
 and can be used to produce a conversational user interface using a continuous dictation (or -domain) speech recognition engine. Frame based grammars parse the recognition results looking for matches to words, word sequences, or CFG rules. This is kind of
 a more advanced version of word-spotting, what you might call "phrase-spotting."
\par Here's an example of the power of a frame to parse a recognition result for an airline ticket ordering service. The user speaks:
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar\adjustright {\i\cf1 I want a ticket from Buffalo to Seattle leaving at six fifteen A. M. please.}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The frame parser accepts the }{\b SRPHRASE}{ structure from the recognizer and will parse out the important information:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 Error! Reference source not found. ToCity=SeaTac LeaveAt=6:15}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice how most of the words are ignored, because they do not add meaning to the parse.
\par The first part of the parse is "\\\\BookFlight" , which gives the name of the frame. The grammar might have multiple frames active (you can think of a frame as a query), 
and find a parse that matches more than one. This parse shows a "frame" used to book a flight, but there also might be a frame used to order a taxi, get a train ride, check the arrival/departure of a flight, etc., all in the same grammar. Applications spe
cify which frames are available by entering a [Frames] section. (Application can only use a [Frames] section if the grammar type is "frames".)
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 [Frames]
\par =\{BookFlight\}
\par =\{BookTaxi\}
\par =\{BookTrain\}
\par =\{CheckArrivalDeparture\}}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The next section of the parse contains several chunks of data. These are called "slots". Each slot is a rule that is searched for within the parse. A slot is just a piece of information, like a number, time, date, or key word. The application needs to spe
cify a set of slots for each frame. Here's an example of what \{BookFlight\} could look like:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 [\{BookFlight\}]
\par =[opt] airline ticket
\par ="FromCity=" from (City) " "
\par ="ToCity=" to (City) " "
\par ="LeaveAt=" leaving at <time> " "
\par ="ArriveAt=" arriving at <Time> " "
\par ="LeaveDate=" leaving on <date> " "
\par ="ArriveDate=" arriving on <date> " "
\par ="UnassociatedTime=" <time> " "
\par ="UnassociatedDate=" <date> " "}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that there are slots that the user didn't specify. The frame based grammar doesn't require that the user specify al
l the slots, nor does it specify what order they're spoken in. So, the user could have said, "Leaving at six fifteen A. M. I want to fly from Buffalo to Seattle" and gotten the same information out. (The order of the parse string would be different, and t
he "LeaveAt=6:15" will appear before "FromCity=Buffalo" instead of the end of the parse.)
\par Also notice the "UnassociatedTime" and "UnassociatedDate" slots. If the user didn't specify enough information about the time/date for the parser to know what he/she m
eant, the unassociated parses of time/date would be returned instead of the leave/arrive versions. The parse knows which version to use because it sees the "leaving at" or "arriving at" keywords. If they don't appear, the parser will choose the unassociat
ed slots. This happens because the parser selects the slots that ignore the fewest words.
\par Another powerful aspect of the frame parser is its ability to have more than one frame in a parse. For example, if the user said:
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar\adjustright {\i\cf1 I want to fly to Seattle and then get me a taxi to downtown when I'm there}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The parse will be
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 \\\\BookFlight:ToCity=Seattle \\\\BookTaxi:Dest=Downtown}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {So, you can see that frame parsing is very powerful if you're designing a conversational system using a continuous dictation engine.
\par }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Microsoft Speech API 4.0}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Log Text File Format}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Applications can view the logged data by running the LOGVIEW.EXE application in the SDK, or by double clicking on a log file (.stl) in explorer. The log viewer shows all of the l
ogged messages and when they were logged. It even plays recordings of the session. Application developers can trace an individual user's progress through the call, hear what they said, and find out what telephony UI needs improvement.
\par If an application designer needs to get statistical information he/she can write tools to analyze a number of log files. Application designers might want to collect statistics on:
\par Intentions -- What do most users call the IVR system for? If the application is a banking application, are most users getting their balance or transferring money? 
\par Success rates -- How many users accomplish the task, and how many hang up before they've accomplished a task? What accuracy is the speech recognition getting for a specific prompt? 
\par Time to complete -- How long does it usually take to complete a specific task? Do expert users complete the tasks quicker than novice users? 
\par The log file format is simple. It consists of:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 GUID CLSID_STLog;         // identifies the header
\par 
\par //Series of log entries. Each log entry consists of:
\par 
\par DWORD dwEntrySize;        // total size for the entry, including
\par                           // this value
\par DWORD dwSizeData;         // size of the data for this entry.
\par                           // 0 if there's no data
\par FILETIME ft;              // file time when the event occurred
\par WORD wDetail;             // detail level of the message
\par WCHAR szSource[];         // source string. NULL terminated
\par WCHAR szMessage[];        // message string. NULL terminated.
\par BYTE abData[dwSizeData];  // data
\par BYTE abFiller[];          // filler data that means nothing.
\par                           // It's used to DWORD align the entry
\par                           // and is included in dwEntrySize.}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can find functions for extracting the log information from the LOGVIEW.EXE sample source code.
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Microsoft Speech API 4.0}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Developing A SAPI-Compliant Speech Engine}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This section contains information for developers of speech recognition and text-to-speech eng
ines. It describes how to develop an engine that is compatible with the Microsoft Speech API 4.0.
\par Before reading this section, you should read "DirectSpeechRecognition API Reference" and "DirectTextToSpeech API Reference" 
\par \page }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Microsoft Speech API 4.0}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Developing A Speech Recognition Engine}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This section contains information to help you develop a speech recognition engine that is compatible with the speech API.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Porting}Porting an Existing speech recognition Engine}{{\*\bkmkend Porting}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Typically, an engine developer ports the code for an existing speech recognition engine from 16-bit Windows, UNIX\'ae, Macintosh\'ae, or OS/2\'ae
, rather than developing a completely new engine. Porting a speech recognition engine to the speech API usually involves the following steps:
\par Port the engine code to Win32 by using the API that is already in the code. This should be straightforward, but be aware that this may uncover old bugs because Win32 is more stringent about memory usage. 
\par Move the engine code into a dynamic-link library (DLL). 
\par Implement the major interfaces for speech recognition first to get the engine running with the speech API as quickly as possible. 
\par Redesign the engine's audio input and output architecture to use str
eams. Using streams makes the engine consistent with the multimedia audio source and multimedia audio-destination objects, which also use streams. These objects are similar to multimedia wave-in and wave-out drivers. 
\par Design the engine's interface architecture to support the interfaces defined for the speech recognition engine object. 
\par Implement the speech recognition interfaces and functions for the engine. 
\par Write additional code to enable the engine to support multiple instances. The time required to do this varies depending on the engine's internal architecture. 
\par The engine must provide the engine enumerator, engine object, and grammar object, and may optionally provide a speech recognition results object. The following table lists the required and optional interfaces for each object.
\par The engine should support both ANSI and Unicode implementations of each interface.
\par Object Interface Engine enumerator }{\b ISREnum}{ }{\b ISRFind}{ (optional) Engine ILexPronounce (optional) }{\b ISRAttributes}{ }{\b ISRCentral}{ }{\b ISRDialogs}{ }{\b ISRSpeaker}{ (optional) Grammar }{\b ISRGramCFG}{ (context-free grammar) }{\b 
ISRGramCommon}{ }{\b ISRGramDictation}{ (dictation grammar) Speech recognition results (optional) }{\b ISRResAudio}{ (optional) }{\b ISRResBasic}{ (optional) }{\b ISRResCorrection}{ (optional) }{\b ISRResEval}{ (optional) }{\b ISRResGraph}{ (optional) }{
\b ISRResMemory}{ (optional) }{\b ISRResMerge}{ (optional) }{\b ISRResSpeaker}{ (optional) 
\par The engine should also support the following notification sinks to pass information back to the application.
\par ISRNotifySink. Used by an engine object to notify the application of interference, noise, and other factors that affect speech recognition. 
\par ISRGramNotifySink. Used by a grammar object to notify the application about a particular recognition.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Registry}Registry}{{\*\bkmkend Registry}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When the user installs the speech recognition engine, the setup program should write the following registry entries:
\par A class identifier entry for each OLE Component Object Model (COM) object. 
\par An engine identifier entry under the following key: 
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li1080\ri1080\nowidctlpar\tx0\jclisttab\tx720\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\outlinelevel0\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Voice\\SpeechRecognition\\Engine}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\line The engine identifier entry should be in the following format: 
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li1080\ri1080\nowidctlpar\tx0\jclisttab\tx720\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\outlinelevel0\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 EngineName : REG_SZ : EngineGUID}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\line In an application, the speech recognition enumerator enumerates all of the strings under this key and allows applications to access the engines' speech recognition modes. 

\par Registry entries for any custom marshaling code. 
\par }{\b For more information}{ about class identifiers and registry entries for marshaling code, see the Microsoft Win32 Software Development Kit (SDK). 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Supporting Multiple Engine Instances}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It is important that an engine support multiple instances, both within the same process and within multiple processes, for the following reasons:
\par If multiple instances are not supported, one application may preclude another from using speech recognition. 
\par Speech recognition may be occurring simultaneously from multiple sources, such as the user's computer, one of several telephone lines, and multiple files.
\par The speech recognition engine is an OLE COM object based on a DLL. This means that it uses the process and memory of the executable module that calls it.
\par In general, each engine object should be independent of any other objects created by the DLL. However, some data should be shared to reduce memory consumption. 
\par To minimize memory usage and start-up times, all instances should share common databases, such as lexicons and speaker-independent models. In addition, because the instances are in the same process
 space, they can share memory. However, make sure the instances cooperate when they share data, especially if they modify it. Note also that the instances may be running under separate threads, so mutual exclusion calls will be necessary.
\par You should employ the following protection schemes:
\par Changing a run-time attribute for one instance should not affect other instances. 
\par Enabling, disabling, pausing, or resuming one instance should not affect other instances. 
\par One system user (as logged on) should not be able to affect the engine preferences for another system user.
\par After all of the engine objects are released, the DLL should free any shared memory used among the instances. 
\par }\pard\plain \s81\li360\ri360\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\adjustright {\b\cf1 For more information}{\cf1 \~\~\~about sharing a speech recognition engine among multiple applications, see DirectSpeechRecognition API Reference.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Supporting Multiple languages}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To support multiple languages, an engine should:
\par Provide one or more speech recognition modes for each language, each with its own globally-unique identifier (GUID). If the engine can support multiple languages at one time, all of the modes should have the same engine GUID. 
\par Load the modes for a language when the application uses that particular language. 
\par Do not provide a mode for a language if the application's setup program does not install a mode for that language. 
\par In some cases, one or more applications will need to have several languages active and simultaneously recognizing speech from the same audio source. This is especially true in multilingual countries or countrie
s that do not have localized versions of all products.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Suggested}Suggested Waveform-Audio Formats to Support}{{\*\bkmkend Suggested}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It is strongly recommended that a speech recognition engine support the following waveform-audio formats:
\par 11 kilohertz (kHz), 8-bit mono pulse code modulation (PCM). Every sound card can support this format. 
\par 11 kHz, 16-bit mono PCM. Most sound cards can support this waveform-audio format. 
\par 8 kHz, 8/16-bit mono PCM. Telephony cards can output this format.
\par An engine can also support the following waveform-audio formats:
\par 16 kHz or 22 kHz, 8/16-bit mono PCM. The higher fidelity increases accuracy. 
\par Custom formats. Supporting one or more custom formats allows an engine to take advantage of digital signal processing (DSP) hardware that has waveform-audio drivers that support the custom formats.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Using}Using a DSP to Process Information}{{\*\bkmkend Using}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Microsoft highly recommends that engines be capable of recognizing speech without requiring any assisted hardware, while being able to take advantage of such hardw
are if it is present.
\par A speech recognition engine can use a DSP in any of the following ways, singly or in combination:
\par The DSP board has a wave-in driver written for it, supporting a custom waveform-audio format that represents the normal output of the DSP (some sort of fast Fourier transform (FFT), for example). All engines call the }{\b IAudio}{
::WaveFormatSet member function one or more times when they start, usually going through different PCM types, such as different sampling rates and bits per sample. The engine could also query to see if the waveform-audio device supports the "custom" forma
t (FFT, for example) and use that data format if the device supports it. This may not provide enough of a communication path between the DSP and engine, and it requires that the wave-in driver be written by the engine vendor. 
\par The DSP board has a wave-in dr
iver written for it. The engine can query the IAudioMultiMediaDevice::DeviceNumGet member function to determine the waveform-audio device to which it is connected. If the device number of that waveform-audio device is known to be a friendly DSP board, the
 engine can begin communicating with the DSP directly, or it can go through the wave-in custom messages. The disadvantage of this is that it requires the wave-in driver be written by the engine vendor. 
\par The DSP board provides an API that takes in PCM data and asynchronously outputs FFT data, for example. If the engine gets PCM data, it tries to pass the PCM data down to the DSP to have it do the processing. The disadvantage of this is that a lot of data 
passes back-and-forth across the bus, but it allows the DSP to be used independent of the origin of the audio. 
\par The engine vendor writes a custom audio-source object that communicates directly with the computer. This allows maximum flexibility, but it prevents the engine from being used by applications not specifically written for the engine. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Preventing}Preventing the Exclusive Capture of the Wave-In Device}{{\*\bkmkend Preventing}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The speech recognition engine should have the audio source active only while it needs audio,
 so that if it's attached to a wave-in device, other applications that need the device will not be prevented from using it. To do this:
\par Call the }{\b IAudio}{::Start member function only when a grammar is active and listening. 
\par Call the }{\b IAudio}{::Stop member function when all of the grammars have been deactivated or have stopped listening.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Handling}Handling Echo Cancellation}{{\*\bkmkend Handling}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If an engine is recognizing speech input from the computer's microphone while speaking output over an audio speaker on that computer, th
e engine may recognize its own output as input. The same problem occurs over telephone lines. However, the engine can act as if the computer's output signal has been removed and echo-cancellation has been done on any digital-audio data passed to the engin
e. 
\par The audio driver that handles the output to the attached speakers and the input from the microphone prevents this echoing problem by removing the output signal (referred to as echo cancellation). Echo cancellation is best left to the audio driver because:

\par The speech recognition engine should be hardware-independent. If the engine performed its own echo cancellation, it would need to receive the digital-audio stream from the computer's speaker or telephone line. 
\par Every application that plays digital audio
 cannot be expected to forward all audio through the speech recognition engine because multimedia drivers do not guarantee synchronization of the digital input with the digital output. Only the driver can guarantee the synchronization of the analog-to-dig
ital converter (ADC) and the digital-to-analog converter (DAC). 
\par The sampling rate of the output wave may differ from the input source. The volume level and waveform-audio format can also differ. 
\par Even if these issues were resolved, there are other ways to produce audio from a computer, such as with music synthesis.
\par The following mechanisms for echo canceling exist:
\par Assume that the audio source is already echo canceled and that the computer will not be hearing itself. 
\par Because many engines are already workin
g in the frequency domain, some engines may be able to do their own echo canceling if they know what's being summed with the input channel. Engines that can do this should invent a new waveform-audio format and register it with Multimedia Systems. The wav
eform-audio format should contain two channels, one with the input signal and the other with the signal being summed/output to the channel. 
\par When an engine starts, it tries to call the }{\b IAudio}{::WaveFormatSet member function with the custom format. If the har
dware drivers that the engine is connecting to can handle the format, the drivers can return the echo information. Audio sources that cannot provide echo-canceling features return an AUDERR_WFNOTSUPPORTED error. The engine can then attempt to open the dev
ice with a more traditional format, like PCM mono.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Supporting Scaleable Platforms}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It is highly recommended that a speech recognition engine be scalable so that it can work with different processor speeds and memory configurations. This not only broadens the
 engine's market, but it provides experienced users with the ability to make tradeoffs between recognition accuracy and demands for processor resources.
\par When an engine is first installed or loaded, it should analyze the machine and make certain tradeoffs f
or optimal performance. To run effectively on slower machines, an engine should accept a higher error rate in recognitions. To take advantage of faster machines, the engine should:
\par Use threads so the engine runs on symmetric multi-processor (SMP) machines that are running with Windows NT. 
\par Take advantage of a DSP if one is available.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Supporting}Supporting Recognition Over the Telephone}{{\*\bkmkend Supporting}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A speech recognition engine should support recognition over the telephone line as well as from a wave-in audio device connected to the computer. Here are a few suggestions:
\par Supply at least one speech recognition mode that supports an 8-kHz sampling rate. 
\par Support multiple instances of the engine while recognizing input from different sources. This allows recognition t
o occur over the computer and the telephone line at the same time, or several telephone lines can be connected to the same machine, all of them active at the same time. 
\par Set the flag in the }{\b SRMODEINFO}{ structure that indicates that the mode is designed for the telephone.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Connecting}Connecting Directly to Audio Hardware}{{\*\bkmkend Connecting}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
An existing engine may connect directly to audio hardware, usually to a circuit board with a hardware assist. If necessary, an engine can connect directly to the hardware and still be
 ported to the speech API. Be aware, however, that the engine will work only with applications specifically designed to cooperate with that engine, which limits the usefulness of the engine.
\par To enable an engine to connect directly to audio hardware:
\par Create a custom OLE interface -- for example, ICustom -- that provides direct control of the hardware. 
\par Provide a custom OLE object -- for example, "custom audio source" -- that supports the ICustom interface. 
\par To enable an application to recognize audio directly from the hardware:
\par Create an instance of the custom audio-source object and pass the instance to the engine. 
\par When the application passes a pointer to an }{\b IUnknown}{ interface to the engine, the engine calls the }{\b IUnknown}{::QueryInterface member function for the ICustom interface. 
\par If the ICustom interface is found, the engine uses ICustom to control and configure the hardware. 
\par If the ICustom interface is not found, the engine uses the digital-audio data supplied by the }{\b IAudio}{ and }{\b IAudioSource}{ interfaces. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Managing}Managing User Preferences}{{\*\bkmkend Managing}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {One system user should not be able to affect the preferences, lexicon, and other settings of another user. This is especially true for an engine running on Windows NT. 
\par The speech recognition engine is responsible for handling all user preferences that cannot be set with the standard API. To do this, an engine should store registry values under the HKEY_CURRENT_USER key. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Maintaining}Maintaining a Database of Compiled Grammars}{{\*\bkmkend Maintaining}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The speech API was written with the assumption that applications, rather than the engine, will store compiled grammars for the following reasons:
\par The application can determine when the compiled grammars are no longer needed and can free them. 
\par The engine doesn't need to store a database. 
\par The compiled grammar can be transferred to another machine.
\par It may be preferable for some engines to store compiled grammars, especially if they include user-specific information in the grammar. To store compiled grammars, the engine can maintain 
a database of grammars. Instead of filling in the native grammar with a representation of the grammar, it can store a handle to the grammar. 
\par When the application eventually passes back the handle of the native grammar, the engine uses the handle to access
 its database. This handle should be robust enough to be transferred to another machine and recognized as foreign by the engine on the other machine. In addition, the engine should be able to determine whether the handle was deleted because the engine's d
atabase was too large.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Defining}Defining Additional Interfaces}{{\*\bkmkend Defining}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
An engine can provide additional functionality to an application by supporting custom interfaces. These interfaces can expose features that the speech API does not yet support. To support a custom interface: 
\par Define an interface that follows the OLE COM and that contains all of the functions necessary to support the additional functionality. 
\par Provide marshaling for the interface so the engine can run in a different process than the calling application. 
\par Enable the engine to return a pointer to the interface when the engine receives a call of the }{\b IUnknown}{::QueryInterface member function for that interface. 
\par Provide documentation for the interface so that application developers can take advantage of it.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Distributing}Distributing Processing Over a Network}{{\*\bkmkend Distributing}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An engine might want to do some of its processing on a different machine than the one the application is running on. While the Microsoft Speech API provides no official way of imple
menting the distributed processing, it's fairly easy for an engine to distribute processing.
\par To distribute processing, split the engine into two parts.
\par The first part is basically a stub that takes all of the calls to the speech API and marshals the data o
ver the network, to the second part. The engine stub supports all of the interfaces that the full engine supports, although it does almost none of the processing. In order to reduce network usage, the stub might do feature extraction on the host.
\par The second part of the engine lies on a server. It accepts the marshaled data from the stub and then processes it, sending back notifications to the stub as recognitions occur.
\par The engine might also support a custom interface, IMyDistri
buted. The IMyDistributed interface allows an application that knows about remote processing to control which machine the processing is on, and to finely control what kind of data is transmitted over the LAN (or other communication method). In this way, t
he application can minimize transmission of unneeded data.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Getting}Getting a Fake Engine from Microsoft}{{\*\bkmkend Getting}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Developers who are converting their engine to the Microsoft Speech API can write to MSSpeech@Microsoft.Com for the source code to a "fake" speech r
ecognition engine that works with the API.
\par Debugging the Engine in Applications that Use Voice Commands/Dictation
\par To debug the engine in applications using voice text:
\par Open Visual C (or whatever debugging environment you're using) with the engine DLL. 
\par Set the application to run as "$(WINDOWS)\\Speech\\VCMD.EXE -Embedding". $(WINDOWS) should be replaced with your windows directory. 
\par Set a breakpoint in the engine's class factory so that you know that the debugging is working. 
\par Start the debugging session. This will run VCMD.EXE. The debugger will be running although the engine won't be loaded yet. 
\par Run the application that uses Voice Commands or Voice Dictation. 
\par Execution should hit your engine's breakpoint. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Low-Level API Calls used by the Speech Recognition Enumerator}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The speech recognition enumerator uses the following interfaces and functions of the DirectSpeechRecognition API.
\par ISREnumW Member Function Next Skip Reset Clone Select 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Low-Level API Calls used by Voice Commands}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The high-level Voice Commands AP
I works with any speech recognizer that supports continuous context-free grammars. It uses the following interfaces and functions of the DirectSpeechRecognition API:
\par ISRAttributesW Member Function AutoGainEnableGet/Set MicrophoneGet/Set SpeakerGet/Set Thre
sholdGet/Set TimeOutGet/Set ISRCentralW Member Function GrammarLoad Context-free grammars. Some "words" have numbers and other symbols. ModeGet Register Resume UnRegister ISRDialogsW Member Function AboutDlg GeneralDlg LexiconDlg TrainGeneralDlg TrainMicD
l
g ISREnumW Member Function Next Skip Reset Clone Select ISRGramCommonW Member Function Activate Archive Deactivate DeteriorationGet DeteriorationSet TrainDlg ISRGramCFGW Member Function ListAppend ListGet ListQuery ListRemove ListSet Some list entries may
 
have multiple words. ISRGramNotifySinkW Member Function PhraseFinish PhraseStart Training ISRNotifySinkW Member Function AttribChanged Interference Sound UtteranceBegin UtteranceEnd VUMeter ISRResAudioW GetWAV ISRResBasicW FlagsGet Identify PhraseGet Time
Get ISRResAudio ISRResBasic ISRResCorrection ISRResEval ISRResGraph ISRResGraphEx ISRResMemory ISRResMerge ISRResModifyGUI ISRResScores ISRResSpeaker Results objects can be exported to the application, so any results object can be accessed. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-440\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \fs40\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 {\*\bkmkstart Low}Low-Level API Calls used by Voice Dictation}{{\*\bkmkend Low}
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The high-level Voice Dictation API works with any speech recognizer that supports discrete/continuous dictation or limited-domain grammars. It uses the following interfaces and functions of the DirectSpeechRecognition API
\par ILexPronounceA/W Member Function Add Get Remove ISRAttributesW Member Function AutoGainEnableGet/Set EchoGet/Set EnergyFloorGet/Set MicrophoneGet/Set RealTimeGet/Set SpeakerGet/Set ThresholdGet/Set TimeOutGet/Set ISRCentralW Member Function Pause GrammarL
oad ModeGet PosnGet Register Resume ToFileTime UnRegister ISRDialogsW Member Function AboutDlg GeneralDlg LexiconDlg TrainGeneralDlg TrainMicDlg ISREnumW Member Function Next Skip Reset Clone Select ISRGramCommonW Member Function Activate Archive Deactiva
t
