\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Generally, the Voice Dictation module will transcribe exactly what the engine passes up to it. However if it sees a backslash in a "word" then it puts whate
ver is to the left of the back-slash in the text buffer. This would be used for punctuation, like:
\par }\pard\plain \s83\li360\ri360\nowidctlpar\tx0\jclisttab\tx720\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631\adjustright \f2\fs20 {\cs82\cf1 \tab .\\Period
\par \tab ;\\Semi-colon
\par \tab \\\\Back-Slash}{\cf1 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If a number is passed up (because the recognizer supports continuous digits) then the number is added to the text buffer without alteration.
\par }{\cs80\v\cf6 <!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//IETF//DTD HTML//EN">}{\fs28 Microsoft Speech API 4.0}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\li-360\sb120\sa120\sl-560\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \fs52\kerning28\cgrid {\cf1 Required And Optional SR Interfaces}{
\par }\pard\plain \s16\sa120\sl-240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The following interfaces are required/optional for SAPI 4.0 engine
s. In order for a speech recognition engine to claim "SAPI Compliant" it must support all of the SAPI interfaces marked as required below. Any engine which supports a subset of required interfaces may claim SAPI compatibility only. Engine developers are s
trongly encouraged to plan to add the interfaces currently marked as "optional", as many will most like be required for compliance in future versions of SAPI:
\par ILexPronounce - off main object (Optional) 
\par ILexPronounce - off grammar object (Optional) 
\par ISRAttributes (Required) 
\par AutoGainEnableGet/Set - Optional 
\par EchoGet/Set - Optional 
\par EnergyFloorGet/Set - Optional 
\par MicrophoneGet/Set - Optional 
\par RealTimeGet/Set - Optional 
